#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""copy_paper.py: a clean reading copy of a paper from the element boxer's outputs.

Usage:  copy_paper.py PAPER_DIR            (PAPER_DIR holds page-NN.json, main-text.json, authors.json)
Writes: PAPER_DIR/copy.md, copy.tables.json (table checks), copy.floats.json (document-wide
float ownership), figures/*.png (200 dpi crops), and copy.hyphens.json (line-join decisions);
copy.trace.json (native provenance: every unit's source rows/runs, recorded when the unit is written, and
every printed line's one destination) and copy.checks.json (code checks, also listed under uncertainty).
Exit status 3: the copy is written, but its trace lists lines in no unit or in two units (tools/TRACE.md).

The copy holds, in order: the title, the authors with their affiliation markers, the affiliations, then the text in
reading order (headings marked "##", paragraphs rejoined across column and page breaks, line-end hyphenation undone),
each table (caption, then one row per printed row, cells separated by " | ") and figure caption after the first
paragraph citing it on its page, or that page's first paragraph. Every boundary of the body stream is decided
by rule 9's one boundary witness (element_boxer_sc.py::boundary_witness): two pieces join on a text witness
(lowercase or hyphen start, closed bracket) or a layout witness (next line, column turn), never on an
unfinished end alone; a set-apart block between an unfinished end and a lowercase resume leaves the sentence
(lift_inserted_blocks); article-information rows that belong to a body sentence return to it; and no float,
caption, note or side block is left between the halves of a sentence (witness_slots; copy.checks.json
sentence_slots lists any that remain). A top-of-column float precedes new prose
and follows any paragraph continuing beneath it. Floats wait until an introduced list finishes;
Figure caption runs belong only to their float, even when also classified as main text. A separate abstract
column follows its heading before body prose, using its printed extent rather than the body column order.
Structured abstract ownership from rule 6 prevents front-matter trimming. Tall initials
are attached to their first ordinary baseline; mixed character rows are separated first.
OCR initials captured with a later line move to the first indented line without inventing text. Column-foot note runs
wait for sentence continuation across pages. Drop caps use verified ordinary-letter geometry
for row order and spacing. Repeated row assembly keeps justified list items whole.
Within a block, a list marker starts an item only after sentence punctuation or a lead-in colon;
marker-looking continuations remain in their paragraph, before its page marker.
Aligned consecutive markers with hanging continuations define separate item blocks even
when the boxer grouped an unpunctuated list into one block.
Non-article documents begin with an inferred document-kind line, also disclosed under uncertainty.
Unordered prose uses the same native-character row reader as main text when short
upright groups or sustained multiword rows establish prose. Collections dominated
by short graphic labels keep their geometry-only row reader. Short numeric
continuations remain printed when a neighboring ordinary row proves their position.
A caption below a spanning picture may continue in a second aligned prose column;
matching type, substantial rows and an unfinished sentence establish column order.
An already owned caption with a sustained empty gutter reads down each column.
Unpaired figure labels own adjacent same-size caption text and its tightly
wrapped lines, without borrowing the neighboring body column. A labelled
glossary with aligned bold terms and longer definitions reads one entry per
term; matching next-page columns retain an unfinished definition with its term.
The glossary heading and preceding section retain their printed order.
Complete parenthesized journal records ending a summary are article information.
Rule 8's publication headers and access badges are article information too. Marked
addresses recovered from an explicit later author-details panel appear once with
the byline and their source page; repeated front-page addresses are deduplicated.
Keyword-witnessed side abstracts retain their own region before the body. Translation
bands are independent sections after references, in authors/title/abstract/key-word order.
A spanning keyword suffix does not merge the sustained prose columns above it.
A run-on keyword list continues in a matching strip above the next page's body.
Consecutively numbered reference columns retain a smaller middle column when
the established outer columns, shared top and typography corroborate it.
An already recovered table-foot note owns its matching nearby prose copy once.
Image-only pages, even with running heads, are declared at their place and under uncertainty.
Raster pages with PDF-proven OCR layers use a separate conservative copy path. An
inset raster may cover 80% of a page when visibility evidence proves the hidden
text layer. Confirmed OCR sections also use this path inside a mixed document.
Distinct article-opening DOI headers with display titles and abstracts declare
a compilation. Numbered references above a later first-page title are explicitly
labelled as the previous article's information, preserving their column order.
Source baselines are retained without spelling, symbol, hyphen or author-name repair.
Disjoint ink rows swallowed by an oversized OCR font are separated using the PDF
characters, retaining every letter. A display title above its byline precedes a
side abstract even when rule 8 missed the title; OCR misspellings remain unchanged.
An uppercase front row with overlapping text-show segments below display text
keeps those segments whole in x order before words are built, recording the reorder.
Repeated margin patterns (including alternate pages, stacked heads and OCR spelling drift),
vertical or one-glyph-wide outer-margin strips, download notices and isolated margin folios
are excluded and recorded. Labelled folios need no recurrence when detached at
the page edge. A coincident duplicate initial over a figure label is separated
using PDF text-show geometry and recorded as an exclusion. Unconfirmed
detached page headers appear once under uncertainty, never at a sentence's page break.
Captioned and geometrically table-like regions are atomic printed-line lists with an
explicit structure warning. Unverified front matter is printed verbatim and flagged.
Native figure bounds or printed figure labels keep their OCR captions together.
Caption columns follow their own gaps and labels, including captions wider than
the body columns. A local caption edge protects narrow body continuations beside
an image. Incomplete figure labels are retained and explicitly flagged, never guessed.
Paired narrow side blocks read one column at a time, outside reference continuations.
Marked small-type column-foot notes wait for an open sentence to finish. A terminal
uppercase abbreviation with sentence punctuation does not become a heading.
A hyphenated caption can continue at the top of the next column of the same
caption band, including a band containing several consecutive captions. Matching
size, alignment and the absence of a new label bound its continuation. Its entry
column anchors placement; the continuation is emitted after that whole column.
Verified PDF character advances split OCR runs across every sustained page gutter;
full-width bands retain their position. Rotated margin letters embedded in
captions are separated before furniture detection. Split pieces retain their
original run IDs and text in the scan audit. Overlapping successive lines do
not merge merely because a tall title or a skewed neighboring column shares
their row. Each numbered bibliography entry starts its own paragraph.
Scan floats wait for a paragraph boundary, including continuations across pages.
Colons and semicolons leave the sentence open across a caption.
Roman and Arabic table captions own their headers and final totals. Numbered
bibliographic columns are protected from table detection.
Captioned tables precede graphic ownership; numeric-only table candidates follow
it, so plot axes cannot combine with neighboring prose into a false table.
The page records retain image coverage, visibility and producer evidence; copy.scan.json
accounts for every OCR run and its destination. The scan conservation ledger checks
those verified source pieces rather than incompatible native run IDs. Born-digital
pages keep the normal path.
Verified raised citation digits assigned to another line return to their native preceding
word when character adjacency and baseline geometry agree; table runs are untouched.
Verified raised citation groups whose numbers occur in the bibliography are bracketed
after their preceding word or value, including detached runs. Table cells and gated
lines use their own reading direction for this evidence, including rotated tables.
Parenthesized publication years in tables or after author names can precede
citations. Powers of ten keep a
caret (10^9); units, charges and ordinal suffixes keep their existing rendering unless
they follow a numeral, where a caret separates them from the value.
Detached lowered digits join their uniquely adjacent formula character before
row grouping, using verified native glyphs and advances. They remain inline;
an underscore and a space distinguish a following ordinary digit. Original
word spaces survive outside the joined formula, and the source runs are recorded.
One printed row is one Markdown row: a cell is fresh on its baseline when it starts
an entry (a complete value, or text opening with a capital or digit whose cell above is
finished, or set at the column margin where the column hangs its wrapped lines); a
lower-case first-column start is a wrapped label only with a wrap witness (a hanging
indent, an unfinished label above, or no fresh cell beside it); a label-only line over
two or more equally indented lines with fresh cells is a group label; a baseline with a
blank first cell whose every occupied cell is fresh is a row of its own, also inside a
study band. A full-width rule with only note lines below it closes a table and never
ends its header; a short marked note under that rule is a note. The structure checks
read the same witnesses from the source baselines. Rows aligned to a captioned table's
column tracks below the detector box, in its smaller cell type, join its gate; a closed
frame covering part of a seed leaves a residual table for the float's other caption; a
table caption whose frame holds no text runs but outline art is declared as such. Body
prose never enters a table's line list: a closing border as wide as the cell rows releases
the body columns below it, a detector seed or swallowed block of body-face sentence lines
beside a table part returns to the column stream, and gate growth never takes such a line.
Tables whose source contradicts their grid instead carry a structure warning and one list item per
printed line, in their own reading frame. copy.tables.json records every check and decision.
Assembly warnings are failures too. Split phrases and first-column entries, unfinished cells,
unlabelled surplus rows, numeric headers and data above a header cannot pass as verified grids.
A gated table then gets one independent reading from its printed layout (the float
mapper's table port, layout_grid): baselines become rows, whitespace gutters recurring on
two or more baselines become columns, and each printed run belongs to the one cell whose
column contains it. Wrapped cells join only with a rule, spacing or text witness; a spanning
header is repeated over the sub-headers it covers; a top-aligned first-column label spans
the blank first cells below it only with a group witness. That grid replaces the gate only
when every printed word is owned exactly once (cell, caption or note) and the same
structure checks pass; otherwise the printed lines stay. copy.tables.json records it.
Sentences continuing across labelled rows must also pass the row-span check. A hanging
paragraph with continuous source baselines and no intervening cell border is kept once
in its first row, with an explicit span through the following empty cells. Ambiguous
sentence spans fail verification and retain their printed lines under the table gate.
The gate retains source baselines, including notes within the region, without cell reflow.
After placement, unowned aligned cell islands join the nearest same-page or
facing-page table caption. Shaded cell divisions or repeated numeric columns
establish ownership; captionless parts carry an explicit continued label.
Caption typography recognizes Arabic and Roman table labels regardless of case
or letter spacing. A side header bounds the whole wrapped caption before it is
attached to a partial grid. Matching facing-page header/data bands and a closing
border recover tall rows as separate printed-line gates under their own table.
On consecutive table-only pages, labelled parts of each table stay together.
Closing borders and the opening cell type release subsequent body columns
back to prose even when most of a detector frame contains ordinary sentences.
The warning counts its actual line-list payload. An empty caption frame explicitly
says that no printed lines were recovered; it does not promise a zero-line table.
An adjacent reference or body list has an explicit boundary after the gated payload.
Caption-only floats also retain their bounded source lines and a warning. Explicit supplemental
tables and ruled continuations keep their lines out of the reference stream; the gate checks
the former block order for detached columns. Entry names after sparse ordinal columns count
as row starts. Caption words repeated below the grid's own border are legitimate header text.
printed top-of-page continuations precede the page's prose without splitting an ongoing paragraph or list.
Continued columns on the same page follow their first part. Table captions beside a grid and undetected
shaded lists keep their caption and cell ownership in the table's own reading frame.
Facing-page columns with only a shaded header use matching first-column row starts and separated
cells to establish ownership; uncertain parts name their page and retain every printed baseline.
Bibliographic first-column citations delimit publication rows, including a single
entry on a continuation page. Country and title lines remain in that entry.
Split-height tiled headers retain all leaf columns. Side-caption tables may
have tall shaded rows; a closing rule or a proven cell column retains the
final unshaded row's wrapped lines before a margin-aligned note.
Side captions use the complete adjacent shaded bands; a neighboring figure cannot reclaim a
captioned table. Undetected rotated tables and their following-page rows use repeated column
starts and retain every baseline under a structure warning. Captionless ruled numeric/symbol
grids retain body-classified cells and follow the last paragraph printed above them. An empty
table output with text inside its declared frame is an explicit gate error, not a zero-sized frame.
Caption panels bound each table's columns and retain indented title continuations. Body-face
sentences filling adjacent prose columns remain body text. Table regions exclude rule-6 furniture;
matching shaded rows can establish that a recurring header or caption is actually table content.
The printed caption and the header below it also prevent page-frame banners from growing into
the table. The sidecar checks caption completeness and body-sentence contamination independently.
Undetected captioned lists keep their rule-7 boundary and printed lines. Changed anchors are recorded in the
page JSON's copy_flow_moves. Non-prose titles, labels and scale bars owned by a
graphic frame, plus panel letters and significance stars within one letter height,
are omitted and recorded in copy_figure_lettering when an earlier body class
would leak them or they sit outside the float's membership. The graphic ink directly
above a figure caption bounds its band: blocks wholly inside it, and one centred
unpunctuated title line just above it, are figure lettering and never join body text.
Every float frame
vetoes heading style before paragraph splitting. A separately styled bare number below
an image of matching width owns its multiline caption and cannot interrupt prose.
References retain whole printed rows within their columns;
consecutive reference systems across columns and pages are checked by the
D-555/D-573 mapper rule and recorded in copy.references.json. One missing
marker may be inferred with a review flag; two consecutive gaps end the
system. Printed labels are never silently corrected. A narrow heading block
containing only detached consecutive labels returns them to their uniquely
aligned author rows before the bibliography boundary is placed.
Unplaced runs inside a reference block's row band join that row at their printed x position,
even when an underestimated column classified them as asides.
Initials keep their x order, and detached accents join the letter their ink overlaps before rows are grouped.
Left-edge labels (including unspaced and right-aligned numbers) or hanging indents start entries. Indented
numeric continuations stay in their entry. A supplement heading closes the reference list, and
aligned reference labels beside author lines cannot form a table. Then come, at the end, the
places the program is not sure of. Running heads, page
numbers and other page furniture are left out. Image-only tables and captionless image floats are
declared unread, with their available captions and an uncertainty entry. Blank row gaps in a cleaned
block retain independent title/heading lines in printed order. Letters, reviewer labels, acknowledgements
and legend sections end the reference stream and cannot join an earlier unfinished paragraph.
Nothing is invented: every content word comes from the PDF's own text, and
every printed word is either placed in the copy, left out as page furniture or figure lettering, or listed at the end.
The final copy is checked against every read run after placement. Any unplaced
run is quoted in full with its page and source id under uncertainty; exclusion
evidence and counts are saved in copy.conservation.json for the PDF-line audit.
An occurrence ledger also consumes actual output spans, so a repeated word or
number cannot satisfy multiple source tokens. Unverified lexical occurrences
are quoted separately under uncertainty. The ledger records its normalization;
it checks multiplicity, while the line audit checks order and glyph checks test values.
Wrapped numeric URL tails with a shared source-block witness override folio
classification. Body columns released by table repair re-enter that page's
reading stream; later tables cannot reclaim those sentences as cell islands.
Colon-term abbreviation lists follow their row pairs across columns and onto
the immediate next page, stopping before the next section or bibliography.
Native white fill is omitted only with an independently white rendered glyph area; visible reversed text stays.
Empty glyph spacers after printed bullets are omitted, and uncertainty about replacement characters is
reported only when a replacement character survives in the delivered copy. Discretionary hyphens are
removed inside lines and resolved as line-end dashes at breaks. Spaced caption digits form one label number.
Heading styles are considered only at paragraph boundaries: ordinary leading inside a paragraph
is not a blank band. Unfinished preceding text followed by a lowercase continuation vetoes
a heading, including a styled block detached by the boxer. Run-in labels retain their existing treatment.
Display heading fragments cannot join body-sized sentences on a shared baseline,
even when a table elsewhere supplies a column wide enough to contain both.
Pieces that each lie in a different, disjoint body column never join on a shared
baseline, even when a boxed panel supplies a page-wide column.
A framed or shaded panel with two or three text columns is read column by column
when each column hands its sentence on to the next (hyphen or lowercase continuation).
Opaque font names can establish a heading face through repeated short heading-only rows before prose.
Detached styled headings and their attached opening sentences precede the next
body block in their printed column; bold/italic face names may end in .B, _B or -B
(and I, BI), so such headings are never collected at a page end. Numbered sections below an abstract panel
restore the opening body column before its continuation and end the keyword strip.
Affiliation text with separately boxed markers is deduplicated by its complete
address span; correspondence remains a separate article-information item.
Spacing accents in non-float text compose only with overlapping native letters. An unmapped accent
also needs an embedded outline proving a hollow ring, paired dots or a diagonal acute/grave stroke.
Detached accents attach before rows are grouped. The exporter interleaves font-separated pieces by
glyph position only with exact readable character evidence on one ordinary baseline and no overlapping ink;
existing intra-run spaces stand and newly adjacent pieces use their advances. Short stacked
fractions outside floats are grouped at their printed rule before prose rows are assembled.
Verified baselines supply subscript/exponent notation. A surrounding glyph-built roof and
tall symbol stay with the fraction: a readable radical becomes sqrt notation; an undecoded
enclosure is retained as a flagged formula with its readable numerator and denominator.
No fraction component can join the next prose line. A stacked fraction with word operands
(a body-text formula: a rule shorter than its column, every character of its runs above or
below the rule within its span or level with it beside it, operands centred, introduced by
'=' or continued by an operator) is written 'head [numerator] / (denominator) tail'; a
full-span line ending in an operator stacked just above the numerator is a wrapped numerator
line. Each operand keeps its own enclosing brackets or gains square ones. Source runs, bounds and replacements
are recorded. These repairs do not change the boxer's blocks, tables, floats or rule 8.
Unmapped list bullets
require repeated leading positions and a single filled circular outline. Bullet item boundaries come
from printed line starts; labelled keyword strips alone split their in-row separators. Publisher citation
instructions remain as a separate final block after references. Prose line joins use closed inflection
families and productive suffixes witnessed in the paper; table line-joining policy is unchanged.
Front matter follows rule 8's first-text-page selection. A title-matching self-citation
remains Article information, and a wide summary between a delayed byline and its
addresses precedes the adjacent body column. Every front-page line has one place:
a repository cover's rows, a journal citation above the title, rule 8's rejected title
rows and a wholly unused title/author block that cites the article are Article
information; label/value date columns read row by row; key words opening the next page
after an abstract-ending front page follow the abstract; rows quoted in the flagged
printed front block are not repeated in Article information; licence/copyright/footer
rows the cleanup drops, lone divider bars and footer folios are recorded furniture;
any other unused title/author block is quoted under its own flag. A white glyph next to a
painted one is judged on its own pixels. Smaller numbered reference typography
keeps earlier body columns and conclusion continuations before the reference heading.
When those body lines rejoin, a prefix witnessed in another unbroken compound supports
its hyphen, subordinate to existing closed-word, inflection and suffix evidence.
Scanned address bands above a labelled abstract retain their printed line order.
Born-digital bylines keep verified names of up to eight words, including names on
successive same-style rows. An invalid parsed entry cannot discard later authors.
If rule 8 is unverified, or the emitted list is shorter than the independent count of
name-like groups on the source byline, a flagged Front matter as printed item also
retains the byline and affiliation rows in page/line order. Parsed authors remain,
except for an explicitly by-introduced unverified panel: its source rows alone
give the names and addresses, without repeating a tentative structured rendering.
Affiliations rule 8 read on a later page are listed after the first page's with a
page label once per page (an end panel labelled Authors and Affiliations, marked rows
continuing on page 2); a labelled end panel is quoted whole from rule 8's recorded runs,
its marker-only blocks joined to the block at their right and its same-size columns read
one at a time, and its rows leave reference and body blocks by their traced run ids. A
body heading, with or without its opening sentence, that rule 6 filed as front matter on
the title page (below the title, byline, affiliations and abstract, with no date, DOI,
e-mail, copyright or publication label) returns to the body before the adjacent column
that continues its unfinished sentence; a heading alone directly above such a block
precedes it. A conjunction left over between printed affiliations is not article information.
Nearby institution blocks and labelled affiliation panels supply address rows even
when rule 8 could not link them to authors. An unmarked first-page foot address
can also be bounded by its institution opening and explicit email ending.
A prose-only candidate box does not
establish a byline and is explicitly left unidentified under the flag.
If publication-history metadata was selected instead, a marked, name-like row
immediately below it in the same block can establish the source byline.
Detached author/address panels at a page foot or on later pages are front matter:
full byline names with institution/correspondence text, or a short signed email
address, identify the panel. Its label and rows move from reference/body ownership
to the same source flag. Translated author biographies remain with their abstracts.
The glyph/sign copy stage also restores complete multi-letter embedded glyph
names when the reader's fallback font encoding kept only their first letter.
The encoding and selected outline names must agree, explicit ToUnicode stays
authoritative, and every run/character ID is revalidated. Prose and private
table characters expand together, retaining the original glyph box and ID.
Verified Symbol-encoding readings of private-use codes (check_fonts.py) are
applied next; no private-use code reaches a copy: any one left is shown as
U+FFFD in runs, table characters and rule 8 strings, and listed per page/font.
Float identifiers collect all labelled parts in page order without changing the table
gates or the prose anchors. Each part retains its source frame, reading angle, caption,
notes and table decision. Figure panels share their numbered figure's record. Native
graphic bounds, excluding caption runs, supply 200 dpi PNG crops and explicit file
locations under the captions. Unpaired source captions and substantial graphics are
listed independently, never assigned an invented caption. Missing PDFs or failed crops
are explicit failures. Reader agreement, when requested, precedes the final table-status
update; only accepted, current-copy agreement can mark a part reader-verified.
The float ledger ports F-006/F-007/F-008 object accounting: every intersecting
object is assigned once or has an explicit reason; crossing objects remain
unassigned and never enlarge the saved region. C-041/C-043/C-044 admit note
lines only when all inked glyph centres lie in one note box. D-586/D-588
direction notices require the bound part's start/end edge, including an
explicit caption child. D-525 accepts a crop by containment of owned visible
ink, not by matching rectangle edges; clipped or incomplete crops are flagged.
Standard library only."""
import json, os, re, sys, glob, collections, statistics, itertools
import hashlib

with open(__file__,'rb') as _source:
    COPY_CODE_SHA256 = hashlib.sha256(_source.read()).hexdigest()

LABEL = re.compile(r"^\s*((?:[A-Z]\s*){3,12}?)\s*(\d+[A-Za-z]?)\b")   # "TA B L E 1", "F I G U R E 2", "TABLE 5"
TABLE_NUMBER = r"(?:\d+[A-Za-z]?|[IVXLCDM]+)"
TABLE_LABEL = re.compile(r'^\s*T\s*A\s*B\s*L\s*E\s+('+TABLE_NUMBER+r')\b',re.I)
LABEL_WORDS = ("TABLE", "FIGURE", "FIG", "SUPPLEMENTARY", "SCHEME", "BOX", "CHART", "PLATE")
SENT_END = re.compile(r"[.?!:]['\")\]’”]*$")
# A cell whose text ends like this is unfinished: the next printed line in that column wraps it (R1).
# A study-design label printed under an author/year continues that reference cell.
TABLE_DESIGN_LABEL = re.compile(r'^(?:Comparative|Prospective|Retrospective|Cross[-‐ ]sectional|Case[-‐ ](?:control|report|series)|Randomi[sz]ed|Controlled|Cohort|Clinical|Pilot)\b', re.I)
TABLE_UNFINISHED_TAIL = re.compile(r'[-‐‑–—,;:/&(\[+±]$|\b(?:and|or|of|the|in|with|to|for|at|by|vs\.?|from|per|on)$', re.I)
# Per-copy witnesses distinguish newly bracketed raised marks from brackets
# already in the source; adding notation must not change existing float flow.
_RAISED_SENTENCE_ENDINGS = set()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- native trace
class Src(str):
    """A copy string that carries the source rows it was read from.

    Native provenance (copy.trace.json): a row is registered when the copy reads
    printed runs into text (prose_row, front rows, OCR lines); its string then
    carries segments (row, start, end), start/end being character offsets in
    that row's text, or (row, -1, -1) when an operation kept an unknown part of
    the row. Joining, concatenation, stripping and slicing keep the segments;
    every other string operation returns a plain str, so an emission site whose
    text was transformed names its sources explicitly. Value, comparison,
    hashing and output are exactly those of str: provenance never changes text.
    """
    def __new__(cls, value='', segs=()):
        self = str.__new__(cls, value)
        self.segs = tuple(segs)
        return self

    def __getnewargs__(self): return (str(self), self.segs)
    def __add__(self, other): return trace_src(str.__add__(self, other), self, other)
    def __radd__(self, other): return trace_src(str.__add__(other, self), other, self)
    def strip(self, *a): return Src(str.strip(self, *a), self.segs)
    def lstrip(self, *a): return Src(str.lstrip(self, *a), self.segs)
    def rstrip(self, *a): return Src(str.rstrip(self, *a), self.segs)
    def replace(self, *a): return Src(str.replace(self, *a), self.segs)

    def __getitem__(self, key):
        value = str.__getitem__(self, key)
        if not isinstance(key, slice): return value
        if not value: return value
        if len(self.segs) == 1 and self.segs[0][1] >= 0 and self.segs[0][2]-self.segs[0][1] == len(self) and key.step in (None, 1):
            row, a, b = self.segs[0]; i, j, _ = key.indices(len(self))
            return Src(value, ((row, a+i, a+j),))
        return Src(value, tuple((row, -1, -1) for row, a, b in self.segs))


_TRACE = dict(rows=[], row_index={}, units=[], uncertain={}, groups=0)
_SENTINEL = re.compile('[\U000F0000-\U0010FFFD]')


class TraceWitness:
    """The shared boundary witness handed to the native trace's copy checks (tools/copy_trace.py)."""
    def __call__(self, last, nxt): return boundary_witness(last, nxt)
    def open_end(self, text): return stream_tail_open(text)
    def resumes(self, text): return stream_resume(text)


def trace_reset():
    _TRACE.update(rows=[], row_index={}, units=[], uncertain={}, groups=0, generated=[],
                  boundary_witness=TraceWitness())


def trace_segs(*values):
    """Ordered, de-duplicated segments of every Src among values (lists included)."""
    out = []
    def visit(v):
        if isinstance(v, Src): out.extend(v.segs)
        elif isinstance(v, (list, tuple)):
            for x in v: visit(x)
    for v in values: visit(v)
    return tuple(dict.fromkeys(out))


def trace_src(text, *sources):
    """text as a Src carrying the segments of sources (unchanged if none)."""
    segs = trace_segs(*sources)
    return Src(text, segs) if segs else text


def trace_partial(text, *sources):
    """text kept an unknown part of its sources' rows (a cut or filtered copy)."""
    segs = tuple(dict.fromkeys((row, -1, -1) for row, a, b in trace_segs(*sources)))
    return Src(text, segs) if segs else text


def trace_row(page, runs, text):
    """Register one printed row the copy read: its page, run ids and read text."""
    ids = []
    for e in runs:
        for s in (e.get('_subscript_sources') or e.get('_formula_sources') or [e]) if isinstance(e, dict) else [e]:
            mid = s.get('id') if isinstance(s, dict) else s
            if mid is not None and mid not in ids: ids.append(mid)
    if not ids or not isinstance(text, str): return text
    text = str(text)
    key = (page, tuple(ids), text)
    row = _TRACE['row_index'].get(key)
    if row is None:
        row = len(_TRACE['rows']); _TRACE['row_index'][key] = row
        _TRACE['rows'].append(dict(page=page, runs=ids, text=text))
    return Src(text, ((row, 0, len(text)),))


def trace_runs_text(page, ids, text, prefix=''):
    """text read from these runs of a page: whole when their letters agree, else a partial claim."""
    wanted = set(ids)
    members = [e for e in page.get('elements', []) if e.get('kind') == 'text' and e['id'] in wanted]
    row = trace_row(page.get('page'), members, text)
    whole = compact(' '.join(e['text'] for e in members)) == compact(prefix + str(text))
    return row if whole else trace_partial(text, row)


def trace_box_runs(page, box, ids=()):
    """Claims for front text: rule 8's run ids, else the runs inside its box (partial)."""
    if ids: return [(page.get('page'), m, False) for m in ids]
    if not box: return []
    return [(page.get('page'), e['id'], True) for e in page.get('elements', []) if e.get('kind') == 'text'
            and box[0]-1 <= e['box_px'][0] and e['box_px'][2] <= box[2]+1 and box[1]-1 <= e['box_px'][1] and e['box_px'][3] <= box[3]+1]


def trace_note_runs(page, ids, statement):
    """Runs named by a statement in the copy's uncertainty list."""
    for mid in ids:
        _TRACE['uncertain'].setdefault((page, str(mid)), statement[:160])


def trace_group():
    _TRACE['groups'] += 1
    return 'g%05d' % _TRACE['groups']


def trace_mark(text, kind, sources=(), group=None, generated=None, runs=(), **extra):
    """Enclose one unit's text in sentinels and register its native sources.

    sources are Src values (row segments); runs are (page, run id, partial)
    claims for text the copy took from known runs without a registered row.
    Units sharing a group are one destination for line accounting (cells of
    one table, authors of one byline, parts cut from one joined text).
    """
    n = len(_TRACE['units'])
    if n >= 0xFFFD: return text
    _TRACE['units'].append(dict(kind=kind, group=group, segs=list(trace_segs(sources)),
                                runs=[list(r) for r in runs], generated=generated, **extra))
    return chr(0xF0000+n) + str(text) + chr(0x100000+n)


def trace_lines(out, start, kind, sources=(), stop=None, **kw):
    """Mark out[start:stop] (its non-blank lines) as one unit, after any Markdown prefix."""
    idx = [i for i in range(start, len(out) if stop is None else min(stop, len(out))) if str(out[i]).strip()]
    if not idx: return
    first, last = idx[0], idx[-1]
    whole = '\n'.join(str(out[i]) for i in range(first, last+1))
    marked = trace_mark('', kind, sources, **kw)
    if not marked: return
    s, e = marked[0], marked[-1]
    prefix = re.match(r'^(?:#{1,6} |\s*- |> )?', str(out[first]))[0]
    out[first] = prefix + s + str(out[first])[len(prefix):]
    out[last] = str(out[last]) + e
    _TRACE['units'][-1]['_source_text'] = whole


def trace_cells(line, kind, group, **extra):
    """Mark each cell of one written Markdown table row (unescaped bars delimit cells)."""
    bars = [m.start() for m in re.finditer(r'(?<!\\)\|', line)]
    if len(bars) < 2: return line
    out = line[:bars[0]+1]
    for column, (a, b) in enumerate(zip(bars, bars[1:]), 1):
        cell = line[a+1:b]; text = cell.strip()
        lead = cell[:len(cell)-len(cell.lstrip())]; tail = cell[len(lead)+len(text):]
        out += lead + trace_mark(text, kind, group=group, column=column, **extra) + tail + '|'
    return out + line[bars[-1]+1:]


def trace_float(out, start, body_start, notes_start, it, caption, fragment):
    """Units of one written float: caption, table cells or printed lines, notes.

    Sources are the float's own native records: caption run ids, the table's
    source/owned run ids (without its caption) and Src notes. Cells and lines
    of one table share its group: the grid places a printed row in several cells.
    """
    page = it['page']
    if it['kind'] == 'figure': body_start += 1; notes_start += 1
    source = it.get('table_source') or {}
    cap_ids = list(it.get('caption_ids') or source.get('caption_ids') or [])
    cap_runs = [(page, m, False) for m in cap_ids]
    line = str(out[start+1]) if start+1 < len(out) else ''
    # The written caption line, after raised-mark rendering: **caption**,
    # optionally followed by the image-only notice.
    bold = re.match(r'^\*\*(.+)\*\*( — the \w+ is an image; not read)?$', line, re.S)
    c = bold[1] if bold else ''
    if fragment or not bold or c in ('Table (no caption)', 'Figure (no caption)'):
        if line: trace_lines(out, start+1, 'notice', stop=start+2, generated='program caption placeholder')
    else:
        # A continuation part without printed caption runs gets an inferred label.
        inferred = (not cap_runs and not trace_segs(caption, it.get('caption')) and
                    re.search(r'\((?:continued|additional columns)[^()]*\)$|no caption|caption not recovered', c, re.I))
        out[start+1] = '**' + trace_mark(c, 'caption', [caption, it.get('caption')], runs=cap_runs,
            generated='program inferred continuation caption' if inferred else None) + line[2+len(c):]
    for k, sc in enumerate(it.get('subcaptions', [])):
        # subfigure panel captions written as the caption's continuation lines claim their own runs
        if start+2+k < len(out):
            out[start+2+k] = trace_mark(str(out[start+2+k]), 'caption', sc['text'], runs=[(page, m, False) for m in sc['ids']])
    table_ids = ({x['id'] for l in source.get('lines', []) for x in l.get('items', [])} |
                 set(source.get('owned_run_ids', []))) - set(cap_ids)
    group = trace_group(); claims = [(page, m, False) for m in sorted(table_ids, key=str)]
    def first_claims():
        nonlocal claims
        value, claims = claims, []
        return value
    row = 0
    for i in range(body_start, min(notes_start, len(out))):
        value = str(out[i])
        if not value.strip() or value.startswith('<!--'): continue
        if value.startswith('[table structure not recovered'):
            trace_lines(out, i, 'notice', stop=i+1, generated='program table fallback explanation'); continue
        if value.startswith('[the table is an image or outline art'):
            trace_lines(out, i, 'notice', stop=i+1, generated='program outline-art table notice'); continue
        if fragment and value == str(fragment):
            out[i] = trace_mark(value, 'caption', fragment, runs=cap_runs); continue
        if value.startswith('| '):
            if re.fullmatch(r'[| :\-]+', value): continue
            row += 1
            out[i] = trace_cells(value, 'table_cell', group, row=row, runs=())
            if claims: _TRACE['units'][-1 - 0]['runs'] = [list(r) for r in first_claims()]
            continue
        if value.endswith('  ') and it.get('box_claims'):
            # A box item claims its own runs (labelled_box_regions).
            ids = it['box_claims'][min(row, len(it['box_claims'])-1)]; row += 1
            out[i] = trace_mark(value[:-2], 'table_line', group=group, runs=[(page, m, False) for m in ids]) + '  '; continue
        if value.endswith('  ') and it.get('text_lines'):
            out[i] = trace_mark(value[:-2], 'table_line', group=group, runs=first_claims()) + '  '; continue
        out[i] = (value[:2] + trace_mark(value[2:], 'table_line', group=group, runs=first_claims())
                  if value.startswith('- ') else trace_mark(value, 'table_line', group=group, runs=first_claims()))
    if it.get('notes') and notes_start+1 < len(out) and str(out[notes_start+1]).strip():
        trace_lines(out, notes_start+1, 'table_note', it['notes'], stop=notes_start+2, group=group)
        if claims: _TRACE['units'][-1]['runs'] = [list(r) for r in first_claims()]


def trace_snapshot(pages):
    """The decoded source pages as export_lines.py sees them (after glyph restoration)."""
    import copy as copy_module
    return [dict(p, elements=copy_module.deepcopy([e for e in p['elements'] if e.get('kind') == 'text']),
                 blocks=copy_module.deepcopy(p.get('blocks', [])), main_text=copy_module.deepcopy(p.get('main_text')),
                 _raised_marks=[], copy_line_order=[]) for p in pages]


def trace_finish(paper_dir, path, pages, scan_document=False):
    """Write copy.trace.json/copy.checks.json and list fired checks under uncertainty.

    A printed line in no unit (and without furniture or uncertainty evidence),
    a line duplicated in two units, or a unit without a source fails the copy:
    the reasons are listed and the command exits with status 3 (COPY_TRACE=0
    skips the trace).
    """
    if os.environ.get('COPY_TRACE') == '0' or not path or not os.path.isfile(path): return
    import importlib.util
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('_copy_native_trace', os.path.join(
        os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), 'tools', 'copy_trace.py'))
    tracer = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(tracer)
    scan_pages = list(pages) if scan_document else [p for p in pages if (p.get('scan_evidence') or {}).get('ocr_layer')]
    source_pages = _TRACE.get('source_pages') or list(pages)
    try:
        report, checks, notices, failed = tracer.build(paper_dir, table_pdf_path(paper_dir), _TRACE, source_pages,
                                                       scan_pages, pages, scan_document)
        tracer.write(paper_dir, report, checks, notices)
        _TRACE['failed'] = failed
    except Exception as exc:
        import traceback
        _TRACE['failed'] = True
        with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f: text = f.read()
        notice = f'- check: the native trace could not be built: {type(exc).__name__}: {exc}'
        with open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f: f.write(text.rstrip('\n') + '\n' + notice + '\n')
        traceback.print_exc(file=sys.stderr)


def trace_strip(text):
    """Remove sentinels; return clean text and unit spans in it."""
    spans = {}; clean = []; at = 0; last = 0
    for m in _SENTINEL.finditer(text):
        clean.append(text[last:m.start()]); at += m.start()-last; last = m.end()
        code = ord(m[0])
        if code >= 0x100000: spans.setdefault(code-0x100000, [None, None])[1] = at
        else: spans.setdefault(code-0xF0000, [None, None])[0] = at
    clean.append(text[last:])
    return ''.join(clean), spans

def trace_run_anchors(text):
    """(page, run id) -> output spans in text of the copy units that cite that source run.

    Units sharing a group are one destination (the cells of one table: the
    table's source runs are claimed by its first cell), so a unit's span is
    its group's whole span. text must end with the traced body (only notices
    are prepended after the trace is stripped). The token ledger claims a
    run's tokens there first.
    """
    body=_TRACE.get('body_text');spans=_TRACE.get('body_spans') or {}
    if body is None or not text.endswith(body):return {}
    shift=len(text)-len(body);rows=_TRACE['rows'];found=collections.defaultdict(list)
    whole={}
    for n,(s,e) in spans.items():
        if s is None or e is None or n>=len(_TRACE['units']) or _TRACE['units'][n].get('group') is None:continue
        g=_TRACE['units'][n]['group'];whole[g]=[min(s,whole.get(g,[s,e])[0]),max(e,whole.get(g,[s,e])[1])]
    for n,(s,e) in spans.items():
        if s is None or e is None or n>=len(_TRACE['units']):continue
        u=_TRACE['units'][n]
        if u.get('group') in whole:s,e=whole[u['group']]
        if e<=s:continue
        cited={(rows[r]['page'],str(m)) for r,*_ in u.get('segs',()) for m in rows[r]['runs']}
        cited|={(c[0],str(c[1])) for c in u.get('runs',())}
        for k in cited:found[k].append([s+shift,e+shift])
    return {k:sorted(v) for k,v in found.items()}


def _json_ok(value):
    try: json.loads(value); return True
    except ValueError: return False


def trace_author_runs():
    """(page, run id) of the source runs the Authors line cites (its 'author' units)."""
    rows=_TRACE['rows'];found=set()
    for u in _TRACE['units']:
        if u.get('kind')!='author':continue
        found|={(rows[r]['page'],str(m)) for r,*_ in u.get('segs',()) for m in rows[r]['runs']}
        found|={(c[0],str(c[1])) for c in u.get('runs',())}
    return found


def copied_word_count(text, pattern=r"[A-Za-z]{2,}"):
    """Words of the copy that are copied from the PDF, for the completeness share.

    Program-written units (trace 'generated': notices, placeholders, labels)
    are not copied words. A rendered grid counts its words as copied words
    once each: a cell repeating its left neighbour or the cell above it is one
    printed spanning cell rendered into each grid position it spans, so a
    table counts the same whether it is a grid or its printed lines.
    """
    body=_TRACE.get('body_text');spans=_TRACE.get('body_spans') or {}
    chars=list(text)
    if body is not None and text.endswith(body):
        shift=len(text)-len(body)
        for n,(s,e) in spans.items():
            if s is None or e is None or n>=len(_TRACE['units']) or not _TRACE['units'][n].get('generated'):continue
            chars[s+shift:e+shift]=' '*(e-s)
    count=0;above=None
    for line in ''.join(chars).split('\n'):
        if not line.lstrip().startswith('|'):
            above=None;count+=len(re.findall(pattern,line));continue
        cells=[c.strip() for c in re.split(r'(?<!\\)\|',line.strip())[1:-1]]
        if all(re.fullmatch(r':?-{3,}:?',c) for c in cells if c):continue
        for c,cell in enumerate(cells):
            if cell and (c and cell==cells[c-1] or above and c<len(above) and cell==above[c]):continue
            count+=len(re.findall(pattern,cell))
        above=cells
    return count


LIST_MARKER = re.compile(r'^\s*(?:[•●◦]\s*|(?:-|\d{1,2}[.)]|[a-z][.)]|\([a-z]\))\s+)')
ABSTRACT_PARTS = re.compile(r"(?<!and)\s(?=(?:Objectives?|Aims?|Background|Introduction|Purpose|Materials and [Mm]ethods|Methods|Results|Conclusions?|"
                            r"Clinical [Rr]elevance|Clinical [Ss]ignificance|Clinical [Ii]mplications|Scientific [Rr]ationale[^:]{0,40}|Principal [Ff]indings|Practical [Ii]mplications|"
                            r"Keywords|KEYWORDS|Key words)\b\s*:?)")
REF_HEAD = re.compile(r"^(references?|bibliography|literature cited|works cited)\b", re.I)
REF_LIST_HEAD = re.compile(r"^List of (?:included|excluded)(?: full[-‐ ]text)? (?:articles|studies)"
                           r"(?: and the reasons? for exclusion)?\s*:?$", re.I)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- geometry
def frame(angle):
    """box -> (u0, v0, u1, v1): u along the reading direction, v down the lines, for text set at this angle"""
    a = round(angle) % 360
    if a == 90: return lambda b: (-b[3], b[0], -b[1], b[2])      # reads bottom to top; lines run left to right
    if a == 270: return lambda b: (b[1], -b[2], b[3], -b[0])
    if a == 180: return lambda b: (-b[2], -b[3], -b[0], -b[1])
    return lambda b: (b[0], b[1], b[2], b[3])


def lowered_digit_runs(runs):
    """Join a detached numeric subscript to its unique native formula neighbor.

    A small digit hull can sit below its formula's row even when its advance
    touches the preceding letter. Require exact text/glyph agreement, smaller
    type, a lowered origin and digit-height ink. Only standalone numeric runs
    move; all other source characters and their order stay put. A nearby
    baseline letter or closing bracket owns the digits, never a word-sized
    gap, a second possible host or a run already identified as a superscript.
    Copies of the runs keep input ownership
    and table character IDs untouched.
    """
    verified = []
    for e in runs:
        if round(e.get('angle', 0)) % 360: continue
        cs = e.get('_chars', [])
        positions = [i for i, ch in enumerate(e['text']) if not ch.isspace()]
        if (not cs or len(cs) != len(positions) or
                ''.join(c['text'] for c in cs) != ''.join(e['text'][i] for i in positions) or
                any(c.get('_mark_unverified') or any(k not in c for k in
                    ('origin', 'size', 'x0', 'x1', 'y0', 'y1')) for c in cs)):
            continue
        verified.append((e, cs, positions))
    attachments = collections.defaultdict(list)
    consumed = set()
    for e, cs, positions in verified:
        if e.get('_superscript'): continue
        if not re.fullmatch(r'\d+', e['text'].strip()): continue
        if any(c['y1']-c['y0'] < .35*c['size'] for c in cs): continue
        if any(abs(c['origin'][1]-cs[0]['origin'][1]) > .1*cs[0]['size'] or
               not -.1*c['size'] <= c['origin'][0]-(a.get('adv_x1') or a['x1']) <= .15*c['size']
               for a, c in zip(cs, cs[1:])): continue
        targets = []
        for host, hs, hp in verified:
            if host is e: continue
            for i, a in enumerate(hs):
                em = a['size']
                if (not (a['text'].isalpha() or a['text'] in ')]}') or
                    not all(c['size'] < .82*em and .15*em < c['origin'][1]-a['origin'][1] < .7*em for c in cs) or
                    not -.1*em <= cs[0]['origin'][0]-(a.get('adv_x1') or a['x1']) <= .12*em): continue
                following = hs[i+1] if i+1 < len(hs) else None
                if following is not None and (following['origin'][0] < (cs[-1].get('adv_x1') or cs[-1]['x1'])-.1*em or
                        abs(following['origin'][1]-a['origin'][1]) > .1*em): continue
                targets.append((host, i, hp, following))
        if len(targets) != 1: continue
        host, i, hp, following = targets[0]
        start = hp[i]+1
        stop = start
        digits = e['text'].strip()
        if following is not None:
            # The saved blank occupied the missing subscript. Close it only
            # when the next printed glyph touches the subscript's advance.
            gap = following['origin'][0]-(cs[-1].get('adv_x1') or cs[-1]['x1'])
            if gap <= .16*following['size']: stop = hp[i+1]
            if following['text'].isdigit(): digits = '_'+digits+' '
        attachments[id(host)].append((i, start, stop, digits, e))
        consumed.add(id(e))
    result = []
    for e in runs:
        if id(e) in consumed: continue
        if id(e) not in attachments:
            result.append(e); continue
        merged = dict(e, _chars=list(e['_chars']))
        if 'char_ids' in e: merged['char_ids'] = list(e['char_ids'])
        sources = list(e.get('_subscript_sources', [e]))
        for i, start, stop, digits, mark in sorted(attachments[id(e)], key=lambda a:a[0], reverse=True):
            merged['text'] = merged['text'][:start]+digits+merged['text'][stop:]
            merged['_chars'][i+1:i+1] = mark['_chars']
            if 'char_ids' in merged: merged['char_ids'][i+1:i+1] = mark.get('char_ids', [])
            sources.append(mark)
        merged['box_px'] = hull(r['box_px'] for r in sources)
        merged['_subscript_sources'] = sources
        result.append(merged)
    return result


def text_rows(runs):
    """Printed text rows, retaining their runs so text and indentation share one geometry."""
    runs=lowered_digit_runs([e for e in runs if not e.get('_accent_consumed')])
    if not runs: return []
    f = frame(collections.Counter(round(e["angle"]) % 360 for e in runs).most_common(1)[0][0])
    rows = []
    small = [e for e in runs if e.get('_superscript')]
    for e in sorted((e for e in runs if e not in small), key=lambda e: (f(e["box_px"])[1] + f(e["box_px"])[3]) / 2):
        b = f(e["box_px"]); cy = (b[1] + b[3]) / 2; h = max(4, b[3] - b[1])
        if rows and abs(cy - rows[-1][0]) <= .45 * max(h, rows[-1][1]):
            rows[-1][2].append(e)
        else: rows.append((cy, h, [e]))
    for e in small:
        b = f(e['box_px'])
        candidates = [(abs(b[1] - min(f(x['box_px'])[1] for x in es)), es) for _, h, es in rows
                      if min(f(x['box_px'])[1] for x in es) - .5*h <= b[1]
                      and b[3] <= max(f(x['box_px'])[3] for x in es) + .15*h]
        if candidates: min(candidates, key=lambda x:x[0])[1].append(e)
        else: rows.append(((b[1]+b[3])/2, b[3]-b[1], [e]))
    return [sorted(es, key=lambda e: f(e["box_px"])[0]) for _, _, es in sorted(rows, key=lambda r:r[0])]


def formula_baseline_text(runs):
    """Read one bounded math baseline, with explicit raised/lowered indices.

    Exact native characters are required. Smaller digits use the full-size
    baseline, never the previous subscript's baseline, to decide their level.
    Long alphabetic words disqualify prose from becoming a fraction operand.
    """
    chars = []
    for e in runs:
        cs=e.get('_chars', [])
        if not cs or ''.join(c['text'] for c in cs) != re.sub(r'\s','',e['text']): return None
        if any(len(c['text'])!=1 or not all(k in c for k in ('size','origin','x0','x1','y0','y1')) for c in cs): return None
        chars.extend(cs)
    if not chars: return None
    chars.sort(key=lambda c:c['origin'][0])
    raw=''.join(c['text'] for c in chars)
    if re.search(r'[A-Za-z]{3,}',raw): return None
    em=max(c['size'] for c in chars)
    baseline=statistics.median(c['origin'][1] for c in chars if c['size']>=.9*em)
    text='';level='';previous=None
    for c in chars:
        ch=c['text'];delta=baseline-c['origin'][1]
        mark=''
        if c['size'] < .82*em and .13*em < abs(delta) < .8*em:
            if not (ch.isdigit() or ch in '+−-') or not text: return None
            mark='^' if delta>0 else '_'
        elif abs(delta)>.12*em: return None
        if c.get('_mark_unverified'): ch='�'
        if mark and mark!=level: text+=mark
        if not mark and level and ch.isdigit(): text+=' '
        if previous and c['origin'][0]-(previous.get('adv_x1') or previous['x1'])>em: return None
        text+=ch;level=mark;previous=c
    return text


def formula_operand(text):
    """One operand of a written fraction, grouped explicitly.

    A whole operand already enclosed by one bracket pair stays as printed;
    any other operand is wrapped in square brackets, so the '/' cannot be
    read as dividing only its nearest term. Spaces inside a bracket edge
    (a tall bracket's side bearing) are closed.
    """
    text = re.sub(r'\s+', ' ', text).strip()
    text = re.sub(r'([(\[])\s+', r'\1', re.sub(r'\s+([)\]])', r'\1', text))
    pairs = {')': '(', ']': '['}; stack = []; whole = bool(text) and text[0] in '(['
    for i, ch in enumerate(text):
        if ch in '([': stack.append(ch)
        elif ch in ')]':
            if not stack or stack.pop() != pairs[ch]: return '['+text+']'
            if not stack and i != len(text)-1: whole = False
    if stack: return '['+text+']'
    return text if whole else '['+text+']'


def word_fraction(rule, eligible, removed, em, frames, columns=()):
    """A stacked fraction whose operands are words (a body-text formula).

    The printed rule is a thin line outside tables and figures, shorter than
    0.85 of its text column (a column-wide rule is a separator, not a fraction).
    Every character of a taking run must be placed: above or below the rule
    within its span (numerator/denominator), or level with the rule beside it
    (a leading 'X =' or a trailing '× 100'); a run with any other character is
    prose and stops the grouping. Numerator and denominator rows adjacent to
    the rule are centred on it. A full-width row stacked just above the
    numerator and ending in a binary operator is a wrapped numerator line.
    The formula must be introduced by '=' (before the rule or ending the row
    just above the numerator) or continued by an operator after the rule.
    Returns (runs, written text) or None; the text is
    'head [numerator] / (denominator) tail' with explicit grouping.
    """
    a, y, b, z = rule['box_px']; mid = (y+z)/2
    if not (3*em < b-a < 30*em and 0 <= z-y < .12*em): return None
    if any(min(b, f[2]) > max(a, f[0]) and f[1]-em <= mid <= f[3]+em for f in frames): return None
    if any(c[0]-em <= a and b <= c[1]+em and b-a > .85*(c[1]-c[0]) for c in columns): return None
    def placed(c):
        cy = (c['y0']+c['y1'])/2
        inside = a-.1*em <= c['x0'] and c['x1'] <= b+.1*em
        if not inside and abs(cy-mid) <= .6*em:
            return 'head' if c['x1'] <= a+.1*em else 'tail' if c['x0'] >= b-.1*em else None
        if inside and y-1.5*em <= c['y0'] and c['y1'] <= y+.15*em: return 'above'
        if inside and z-.15*em <= c['y0'] and c['y1'] <= z+1.5*em: return 'below'
        return None
    parts = {}
    for e in eligible:
        if e['id'] in removed: continue
        cs = e['_chars']
        if ''.join(c['text'] for c in cs) != re.sub(r'\s', '', e['text']): continue
        eb = e['box_px']
        if eb[3] < y-1.6*em or eb[1] > z+1.6*em: continue
        # Over the rule, or a separate run ending/starting right beside it.
        if not (min(b, eb[2]) > max(a, eb[0]) or 0 <= a-eb[2] <= 1.5*em or 0 <= eb[0]-b <= 1.5*em): continue
        kinds = [placed(c) for c in cs]
        if not any(kinds): continue
        if None in kinds: return None
        offsets = [i for i, ch in enumerate(e['text']) if not ch.isspace()]
        segments = []
        for kind, offset in zip(kinds, offsets):
            if segments and segments[-1][0] == kind: segments[-1][2] = offset
            else: segments.append([kind, offset, offset])
        order = ['head', 'above', 'below', 'tail']
        if (len({k for k, _, _ in segments}) != len(segments) or
                [k for k, _, _ in segments] != sorted((k for k, _, _ in segments), key=order.index) or
                {'above', 'below'} <= {k for k, _, _ in segments}): return None
        parts[e['id']] = (e, {k: e['text'][s:t+1] for k, s, t in segments})
    pieces = {k: [(e, text[k]) for e, text in parts.values() if k in text] for k in ('head', 'above', 'below', 'tail')}
    if not pieces['above'] or not pieces['below']: return None
    if len(pieces['head']) > 1 or len(pieces['tail']) > 1: return None
    def row_box(rows): return hull([c['x0'], c['y0'], c['x1'], c['y1']] for e, _ in rows for c in e['_chars'] if placed(c) in ('above', 'below'))
    for rows in (pieces['above'], pieces['below']):
        rb = row_box(rows)
        if abs((rb[0]+rb[2])/2-(a+b)/2) > .2*(b-a) or rb[2]-rb[0] < .5*(b-a): return None
    numerator = sorted(pieces['above'], key=lambda p: p[0]['box_px'][1])
    denominator = sorted(pieces['below'], key=lambda p: p[0]['box_px'][1])
    if len(denominator) != 1 or len(numerator) != 1: return None
    taken = {p[0]['id'] for p in parts.values()}
    # Wrapped numerator lines: whole runs inside the span, stacked directly above.
    def above_row(top):
        found = [e for e in eligible if e['id'] not in removed and e['id'] not in taken and
                 top-1.8*em <= e['box_px'][3] <= top+.1*em and e['box_px'][1] < top and
                 min(b, e['box_px'][2]) > max(a, e['box_px'][0])]
        return found[0] if len(found) == 1 else None
    top = min(c['y0'] for c in numerator[0][0]['_chars'] if placed(c) == 'above')
    while True:
        e = above_row(top)
        if e is None or not (a-.1*em <= e['box_px'][0] and e['box_px'][2] <= b+.1*em): break
        if not re.search(r'[−+–×÷·-]\s*$', e['text']): break
        if ''.join(c['text'] for c in e.get('_chars', [])) != re.sub(r'\s', '', e['text']): break
        numerator.insert(0, (e, e['text'])); taken.add(e['id']); top = e['box_px'][1]
    head = pieces['head'][0][1].strip() if pieces['head'] else ''
    tail = pieces['tail'][0][1].strip() if pieces['tail'] else ''
    introducer = above_row(top)
    if not (head.endswith('=') or re.match(r'[−+–×÷·=-]', tail) or
            introducer is not None and introducer['text'].rstrip().endswith('=')): return None
    group = [e for e, _ in numerator] + [p[0] for p in parts.values() if p[0]['id'] not in {e['id'] for e, _ in numerator}]
    text = ' '.join(t for t in (head, formula_operand(' '.join(t for _, t in numerator))+' / '+
                                formula_operand(denominator[0][1]), tail) if t)
    return group, text


def display_formula_runs(runs, page):
    """Keep a small ruled fraction and its enclosure in one prose-row slot.

    A short horizontal rule must separate wholly contained math-only operands
    within one em above/below. Table/float frames are excluded. An overlapping
    glyph enclosure needs a tall left stroke plus a continuous thin roof;
    arbitrary words, headings, underlines and following prose cannot join it.
    Unknown enclosures are flagged instead of treating their text-layer letter
    claims as words. Original runs remain untouched for source conservation.
    """
    rules=[e for e in page.get('elements',[])+page.get('art',[]) if e.get('kind')=='rule']
    if not rules: return runs
    eligible=[e for e in runs if not round(e.get('angle',0))%360 and e.get('_chars') and
        all(all(k in c for k in ('size','origin','x0','y0','x1','y1')) for c in e['_chars'])]
    if not eligible: return runs
    em=statistics.median(c['size'] for e in eligible for c in e['_chars'])
    frames=[x['box_px'] for x in page.get('tables',[]) + page.get('floats',[])]
    replaced={};removed=set()
    for rule in rules:
        a,y,b,z=rule['box_px'];mid=(y+z)/2
        words=word_fraction(rule,eligible,removed,em,frames,
                            [c for c in (page.get('main_text') or {}).get('columns',[]) if len(c)==2])
        if words:
            group,after=words
            group=sorted(group,key=lambda e:(e['box_px'][1],e['box_px'][0]))
            anchor=group[0];bounds=hull(e['box_px'] for e in group)
            replaced[anchor['id']]=dict(anchor,text=after,_chars=[],char_ids=[],_formula_sources=group,
                _superscript=False,box_px=bounds,run_size_px=em,_faces={})
            removed.update(e['id'] for e in group)
            record=dict(runs=[e['id'] for e in group],before=' '.join(e['text'] for e in group),
                after=after,box_px=bounds,status='flagged' if '�' in after else 'read',rule=rule['id'],
                why='stacked word operands grouped at their fraction rule and written with explicit grouping')
            for key in ('copy_formulas','copy_line_order'):
                records=page.setdefault(key,[])
                if record not in records: records.append(record)
            continue
        if not (.8*em < b-a < 12*em and 0<=z-y<.12*em): continue
        if any(min(b,f[2])>max(a,f[0]) and f[1]-em<=mid<=f[3]+em for f in frames): continue
        contained=[e for e in eligible if e['id'] not in removed and
            a-.1*em<=e['box_px'][0] and e['box_px'][2]<=b+.1*em]
        top=[e for e in contained if y-em<=e['box_px'][1] and e['box_px'][3]<=y+.05*em]
        bottom=[e for e in contained if z-.05*em<=e['box_px'][1] and e['box_px'][3]<=z+em]
        numerator,denominator=formula_baseline_text(top),formula_baseline_text(bottom)
        if not numerator or not denominator: continue
        group=top+bottom
        box=hull(e['box_px'] for e in group)
        # Both operands must be centred under their shared bar. This rejects
        # neighbouring text above/below a short decorative heading underline.
        if any(abs((hull(e['box_px'] for e in part)[0]+hull(e['box_px'] for e in part)[2])/2-(a+b)/2)>.2*(b-a)
               for part in (top,bottom)): continue
        roofs=[]
        for e in eligible:
            if e in group or e['id'] in removed: continue
            cs=e['_chars'];eb=e['box_px']
            if not (a-2*em<eb[0]<=a and b-.2*em<=eb[2]<b+.4*em and
                    box[1]-em<eb[1]<box[1] and box[3]-.2*em<=eb[3]<box[3]+.5*em): continue
            thin=[c for c in cs if c['y1']-c['y0']<.12*em and c['x1']-c['x0']>.1*em]
            tall=[c for c in cs if c not in thin]
            if len(tall)!=1 or tall[0]['x1']>a+.2*em or tall[0]['y1']-tall[0]['y0']<em: continue
            if not thin or any(abs(c['y0']-eb[1])>.1*em for c in thin): continue
            segments=sorted(set((c['x0'],c['x1']) for c in thin))
            if segments[0][0]>a+.1*em or segments[-1][1]<b-.2*em: continue
            if any(v[0]-u[1]>.1*em for u,v in zip(segments,segments[1:])): continue
            roofs.append(e)
        if len(roofs)>1: continue
        after=f'({numerator})/({denominator})'
        status='flagged' if '�' in after else 'read'
        if roofs:
            group+=roofs
            if any(c['text']=='√' for c in roofs[0]['_chars']): after=f'√({after})'
            else:
                after=f'[formula: {after} inside an unread symbol; see PDF as printed]'
                status='flagged'
        if status=='flagged' and not after.startswith('[formula:'):
            after=f'[formula: {after}; unread glyph, see PDF as printed]'
        group.sort(key=lambda e:(e['box_px'][0],e['box_px'][1]))
        anchor=group[0];bounds=hull(e['box_px'] for e in group)
        replaced[anchor['id']]=dict(anchor,text=after,_chars=[],char_ids=[],
            _superscript=False,box_px=bounds,run_size_px=em,_faces={})
        removed.update(e['id'] for e in group)
        record=dict(runs=[e['id'] for e in group],before=' '.join(e['text'] for e in group),
            after=after,box_px=bounds,status=status,rule=rule['id'],
            why='stacked math operands grouped at their fraction rule before prose line assembly')
        for key in ('copy_formulas','copy_line_order'):
            records=page.setdefault(key,[])
            if record not in records: records.append(record)
    return [replaced.get(e['id'],e) for e in runs if e['id'] not in removed or e['id'] in replaced]


def prose_text_rows(runs, page):
    """A tall initial belongs to the first ordinary baseline beside it.

    Verified characters can span multiple lines in one saved run. Separate the
    ordinary baselines before attaching the initial, retaining every character
    once. Glyph advances preserve word spaces. Tables use their own row reader.
    """
    runs=display_formula_runs(runs,page)
    adjusted = []
    for e in runs:
        cs = e.get('_chars', [])
        if len(cs) < 3 or e['angle'] != 0 or not all('size' in c and 'origin' in c for c in cs):
            adjusted.append(e); continue
        size = sorted(c['size'] for c in cs)[len(cs)//2]
        caps = [c for c in cs if c['size'] >= 1.8*size and c['text'].isupper()]
        rest = [c for c in cs if c not in caps]
        if (len(caps) != 1 or not rest or any(c['size'] > 1.3*size for c in rest)
                or caps[0]['x0'] > min(c['x0'] for c in rest)+.3*size
                or (caps[0]['y0'] > min(c['y0'] for c in rest)-.6*size
                    and caps[0]['y1'] < max(c['y1'] for c in rest)+.6*size)):
            adjusted.append(e); continue
        cap = caps[0]; rows = []
        for c in sorted(rest, key=lambda c:(c['origin'][1], c['x0'])):
            if rows and abs(c['origin'][1]-rows[-1][0]['origin'][1]) < .3*size: rows[-1].append(c)
            else: rows.append([c])
        texts = []
        for i, row in enumerate(rows):
            row.sort(key=lambda c:c['x0'])
            box = hull([c['x0'],c['y0'],c['x1'],c['y1']] for c in row)
            if i == 0: row = [cap]+row; box[0] = cap['x0']
            text = ''
            for j, c in enumerate(row):
                if j and c['origin'][0]-(row[j-1].get('adv_x1') or row[j-1]['x1']) > .18*size: text += ' '
                text += c['text']
            if len(rows)==1 and row==cs:
                # Ordinary word spacing in an already ordered line is stronger
                # evidence than approximate glyph hulls (especially in scans).
                text=e['text']
                if rest[0]['origin'][0]-(cap.get('adv_x1') or cap['x1'])>.18*size and not text[1].isspace():
                    text=text[0]+' '+text[1:]
            adjusted.append(dict(e, text=text, box_px=box, _chars=row, _drop_cap_row=True)); texts.append(text)
        page.setdefault('copy_drop_caps', []).append(dict(run=e['id'], before=e['text'], after='\n'.join(texts),
            why='ordinary baselines separate the lines beside a tall initial'))
    return text_rows(adjusted)


def rows_of(runs, page=None):
    """runs -> printed rows (top to bottom in their own reading frame), each a list of cell texts left to right
    (with a page number, each cell text carries its run as a native trace row)"""
    if not runs: return []
    ang = collections.Counter(round(e["angle"]) % 360 for e in runs).most_common(1)[0][0]
    f = frame(ang)
    items = sorted(((f(e["box_px"]), e) for e in runs), key=lambda t: (t[0][1] + t[0][3]) / 2)
    rows = []
    for box, e in items:
        vc = (box[1] + box[3]) / 2; h = max(4.0, box[3] - box[1])
        if rows and abs(vc - rows[-1]["vc"]) <= 0.45 * max(h, rows[-1]["h"]):
            r = rows[-1]; r["cells"].append((box, e)); r["vc"] = (r["vc"] * (len(r["cells"]) - 1) + vc) / len(r["cells"])
        else:
            rows.append({"vc": vc, "h": h, "cells": [(box, e)]})
    return [[(trace_row(page, [e], e["text"].strip()) if page is not None else e["text"].strip())
             for box, e in sorted(r["cells"], key=lambda t: t[0][0]) if e["text"].strip()] for r in rows]


def table_pdf_path(paper_dir):
    """Optional source for character-accurate cell splits. A colocated paper.pdf or COPY_PAPER_PDF supplies it;
    COPY_PAPER_PDF_MAP can name a JSON map from directory basenames to PDFs for a batch of existing boxer outputs.
    Geometry-only reconstruction remains available when the original PDF is not supplied."""
    path = os.environ.get("COPY_PAPER_PDF") or os.path.join(paper_dir, "paper.pdf")
    if not os.path.isfile(path) and os.environ.get("COPY_PAPER_PDF_MAP"):
        with open(os.environ["COPY_PAPER_PDF_MAP"], encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            path = json.load(fh).get(os.path.basename(os.path.abspath(paper_dir)), "")
    return path if os.path.isfile(path) else None


def table_characters(p):
    """Read the boxer's character geometry, with exactly its region/hidden filtering before char_ids are indexed.
    Verify the text before trusting the IDs: old outputs or a different reader must not silently move values."""
    if "_table_chars" in p: return p["_table_chars"]
    p["_table_chars"] = []
    if not p.get("_table_pdf"): return []
    import importlib.util
    name = "_copy_table_reader"
    if name not in sys.modules:
        path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "standalone", "element_boxer_sc.py")
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(name, path)
        mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); sys.modules[name] = mod; spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
    reader = sys.modules[name]
    chars = reader.sc_read_page(p["_table_pdf"], p["page"])[0]
    scale = p.get("params", {}).get("dpi", reader.PARAMS["dpi"]) / reader.PARAMS["dpi"]
    if scale != 1:
        chars = [dict(c, **{k: c[k]*scale for k in ("x0", "y0", "x1", "y1")}) for c in chars]
    b = p["region_box_px"]
    p["_table_chars"] = [c for c in chars if not c["hidden"] and b[0] <= (c["x0"]+c["x1"])/2 <= b[2]
                         and b[1] <= (c["y0"]+c["y1"])/2 <= b[3]]
    return p["_table_chars"]


def source_typography(pages):
    """Keep verified glyph/font evidence beside saved runs, without changing table character IDs.
    The most frequent face (by printed alphabetic character count) is the paper's body face."""
    faces = collections.Counter()
    for p in pages:
        chars = table_characters(p)
        for e in p['elements']:
            if e['kind'] != 'text': continue
            ids = e.get('char_ids', [])
            cs = [chars[i] for i in ids if chars[i]['text'].strip()] if ids and chars and max(ids)<len(chars) else []
            if re.sub(r'\s', '', e['text']) != re.sub(r'\s', '', ''.join(c['text'] for c in cs)): continue
            rejected={s['char_id'] for s in e.get('font_subs',[]) if s.get('status')=='layout-protected' and 'char_id' in s}
            # Font verification rebuilds each run's character view from this
            # shared array. Keep the veto on the native witness through that
            # rebuild, including layout-protected no-op replacements.
            for i in rejected:
                if 0<=i<len(chars):chars[i]['_mark_unverified']=True
            e['_chars'] = cs
            counts = collections.Counter(re.sub(r'^[A-Z]{6}\+', '', c['font']) for c in cs if c['text'].isalpha())
            e['_faces'] = counts; faces.update(counts)
        # A small numeric run above adjacent full-size prose is a citation, not a new printed line.
        rs = [e for e in p['elements'] if e['kind']=='text' and round(e['angle'])%360==0]
        for e in rs:
            if not re.fullmatch(r'\d+(?:[,–-]\d+)*', e['text'].strip()): continue
            eb=e['box_px']; size=e.get('run_size_px',0)
            e['_superscript'] = any(size < .82*r.get('run_size_px',0) and
                r['box_px'][1]-.5*r['run_size_px'] <= eb[1] < r['box_px'][1]+.2*r['run_size_px'] and
                eb[3] < r['box_px'][3] and r['box_px'][0]-size <= eb[0] <= r['box_px'][2]+size
                for r in rs if re.search(r'[A-Za-z]{3}',r['text']))
    body_face = faces.most_common(1)[0][0] if faces else ''
    for p in pages:
        p['_body_face'] = body_face
        for e in p['elements']:
            for c in e.get('_chars',[]):c['_body_font']=re.sub(r'^[A-Z]{6}\+', '', c['font'])==body_face


def styled_face(face, body_face):
    return face != body_face and bool(re.search(r'bold|italic|oblique|small.?cap|(?:^|[-,])(?:sc\d*|bd|it)(?:$|[-,])', face, re.I))


def omit_run_characters(page, run, indices, why, *, overlay=False):
    """Remove only verified character positions, retaining the other saved spaces.

    The private character array keeps its indices so table char_ids stay valid.
    Saved input text is untouched; copy_omitted records the rendering evidence.
    """
    cs = run.get('_chars', [])
    positions = [i for i, ch in enumerate(run['text']) if not ch.isspace()]
    if (len(cs) != len(positions) or len(cs) != len(run.get('char_ids', [])) or
            ''.join(c['text'] for c in cs) != ''.join(run['text'][i] for i in positions)):
        return
    before = run['text']; removed = {positions[i] for i in indices}
    run['text'] = ''.join(ch for i, ch in enumerate(before) if i not in removed).strip()
    groups=[]
    for i in sorted(indices):
        if not groups or i!=groups[-1][-1]+1:groups.append([])
        groups[-1].append(i)
    invisible=[dict(text=''.join(cs[i]['text'] for i in group),
        box=[min(cs[i]['x0'] for i in group),min(cs[i]['y0'] for i in group),
             max(cs[i]['x1'] for i in group),max(cs[i]['y1'] for i in group)]) for group in groups
        if all(all(k in cs[i] for k in ('x0','y0','x1','y1')) for i in group)]
    page.setdefault('copy_omitted', []).append(dict(run=run['id'], before=before,
        after=run['text'], char_ids=[run['char_ids'][i] for i in indices],
        invisible=invisible, why=why))
    if overlay: page['copy_omitted'][-1]['overlay'] = True
    for i in indices:
        cid = run['char_ids'][i]
        page['_table_chars'][cid] = dict(page['_table_chars'][cid], text='')
    kept = [(cid, c) for i, (cid, c) in enumerate(zip(run['char_ids'], cs)) if i not in indices]
    run['char_ids'] = [cid for cid, c in kept]
    run['_chars'] = [c for cid, c in kept]


def record_copy_omissions(page, elements):
    """Keep overlay evidence without exempting visible words underneath it.

    Ordinary unpainted text owns its excluded area. A diagonal watermark crosses
    unrelated text, so its glyph boxes are evidence only, never area exclusions.
    """
    evidence = page['copy_conservation']
    runs = {e['id']: e for e in elements}
    for omitted in page.get('copy_omitted', []):
        e = runs.get(omitted['run'])
        if e is None: continue
        invisible = omitted.get('invisible', [])
        if not invisible and not omitted.get('after'):
            invisible = [dict(text=omitted['before'], box=e['box_px'])]
        key = 'omitted_overlays' if omitted.get('overlay') else 'excluded'
        evidence.setdefault(key, []).extend(
            dict(id=e['id'], reason=omitted['why'], **part) for part in invisible)


def list_overprinted_layers(pages, uncertain):
    """Lane claims R3: the reader leaves out a white text object painted under later glyphs on a white
    background (element_boxer_sc.sc_overprinted_layers, recorded in the page's hidden_layers); each such
    layer is listed once among the places the program is not sure of, with its text."""
    reader = sys.modules.get('_copy_table_reader')
    reason = getattr(reader, 'SC_OVERPRINT_REASON', None)
    if not reason: return
    for p in pages:
        for r in p.get('hidden_layers') or []:
            if r.get('evidence') == reason and r.get('text'):
                uncertain.append(f"page {p['page']}: a white text layer painted under later printed text was left out "
                                 f"({r['dropped_glyphs']} glyphs): {r['text']!r}; check the printed line")


def omit_unpainted_text(pages, front_page=1):
    """Omit demonstrably unpainted text without a publisher-string blacklist.

    Old page records carry neither fill colours nor white-on-white flags. Read
    the native text-showing operators with the existing reader and match their
    paint order to verified characters. Fill-only DeviceGray/RGB/CMYK white is
    omitted only when its entire glyph rectangle (with a pixel margin) also
    renders white. White lettering on images, coloured panels and uncertain
    colour spaces stays. Invisible/clip-only render modes and zero alpha are
    unpainted independently of colour. Rotated pages abstain from raster checks.
    On the front page a white DOI-link underlay can share a run with a later
    painted copy of the identical DOI. Only that later copy's glyph rectangles
    are masked in the white-area test; coloured backgrounds still fail it.
    The original URI, character IDs and paint evidence remain in copy_omitted.
    A zero-height unmapped spacer immediately after a real bullet is empty ink,
    not another bullet or a missing letter. It can be omitted on glyph evidence.
    """
    import subprocess
    import math
    reader = sys.modules.get('_copy_table_reader')
    if reader is None: return
    class PaintReader(reader.SCInterpreter):
        def do_TJ(self, seq):
            start = len(self.sink.texts)
            super().do_TJ(seq)
            for obj in self.sink.texts[start:]:
                obj['fill'] = self.graphicstate.ncolor
                obj['space'] = self.graphicstate.ncs.name
    def white(obj):
        v = obj.get('fill'); space = obj.get('space')
        if isinstance(v, (int, float)): v = [v]
        return (isinstance(v, (list, tuple)) and
                (space == 'DeviceGray' and tuple(v) == (1,) or
                 space == 'DeviceRGB' and tuple(v) == (1, 1, 1) or
                 space == 'DeviceCMYK' and tuple(v) == (0, 0, 0, 0)))
    for p in pages:
        p.pop('copy_omitted', None)
        runs = [e for e in p['elements'] if e['kind'] == 'text' and e.get('_chars')]
        if not runs or not p.get('_table_pdf'): continue
        doc = reader.sc_doc(p['_table_pdf']); pg = doc.pages[p['page']-1]
        _, height, box = doc.page_size(p['page'])
        sink = reader.SCSink(); interp = PaintReader(doc.rsrc, reader.PDFDevice(doc.rsrc), sink, doc, 0)
        interp.sc_start = {'bbox_local': box}
        interp.render_contents(pg.resources, pg.contents, ctm=reader.MATRIX_IDENTITY)
        candidate = {}
        for e in runs:
            for i, c in enumerate(e['_chars']):
                order = c.get('paint_order')
                if not order or not 0 <= order[0] < len(sink.texts): continue
                obj = sink.texts[order[0]]
                if obj['render'] in (3, 7) or obj['alpha'] == 0:
                    candidate[e['id'], i] = 'invisible text render mode or zero alpha'
                elif obj['render'] in (0, 4) and white(obj):
                    candidate[e['id'], i] = 'white fill and white rendered glyph area'
        raster = None
        if any(v.startswith('white') for v in candidate.values()) and pg.rotate % 360 == 0:
            dpi = p.get('params', {}).get('dpi', 150)
            result = subprocess.run(['pdftoppm', '-f', str(p['page']), '-singlefile',
                '-r', str(dpi), '-cropbox', p['_table_pdf']], capture_output=True, timeout=120)
            match = re.match(rb'P6\s+(\d+)\s+(\d+)\s+255\s', result.stdout)
            if result.returncode == 0 and match:
                w, h = map(int, match.groups()); data = result.stdout[match.end():]
                if len(data) == w*h*3: raster = w, h, data, dpi/72
        underlays = front_link_underlays(runs, candidate) if p['page'] == front_page else {}
        # Prove the background for the complete link, not one obscured glyph.
        # An individual white letter may lie wholly inside a later black
        # letter's rectangle even when the link is visible on a dark panel.
        if raster and underlays:
            w, h, data, scale = raster
            groups = collections.defaultdict(list)
            for e in runs:
                for i, c in enumerate(e['_chars']):
                    if (e['id'],i) in underlays: groups[e['id'],c['paint_order'][0]].append((i,c))
            for (run, order), cs in groups.items():
                masks = underlays[run,cs[0][0]]
                x0=math.floor(min(c['x0'] for i,c in cs)-box[0]*scale)-1
                x1=math.ceil(max(c['x1'] for i,c in cs)-box[0]*scale)+1
                y0=math.floor(min(c['y0'] for i,c in cs)+(box[3]-height)*scale)-1
                y1=math.ceil(max(c['y1'] for i,c in cs)+(box[3]-height)*scale)+1
                masked = [(math.floor(c['x0']-box[0]*scale)-1, math.floor(c['y0']+(box[3]-height)*scale)-1,
                           math.ceil(c['x1']-box[0]*scale)+1, math.ceil(c['y1']+(box[3]-height)*scale)+1)
                          for c in masks]
                white_area = (0 <= x0 < x1 <= w and 0 <= y0 < y1 <= h and not any(
                    min(data[(y*w+x)*3:(y*w+x)*3+3]) < 250 and
                    not any(a <= x <= c and b <= y <= d for a,b,c,d in masked)
                    for y in range(y0,y1) for x in range(x0,x1)))
                if not white_area:
                    for i,c in cs: del underlays[run,i]
        for e in runs:
            reasons = collections.defaultdict(list)
            cs_all = e['_chars']
            for i, c in enumerate(e['_chars']):
                reason = candidate.get((e['id'], i))
                if reason and reason.startswith('white'):
                    if not raster: continue
                    w, h, data, scale = raster
                    x0 = math.floor(c['x0']-box[0]*scale)-1
                    x1 = math.ceil(c['x1']-box[0]*scale)+1
                    y0 = math.floor(c['y0']+(box[3]-height)*scale)-1
                    y1 = math.ceil(c['y1']+(box[3]-height)*scale)+1
                    if not (0 <= x0 < x1 <= w and 0 <= y0 < y1 <= h): continue
                    masks = underlays.get((e['id'], i), [])
                    if masks:
                        dark = any(min(data[(y*w+x)*3:(y*w+x)*3+3]) < 250 and not any(
                            math.floor(m['x0']-box[0]*scale)-1 <= x <= math.ceil(m['x1']-box[0]*scale)+1 and
                            math.floor(m['y0']+(box[3]-height)*scale)-1 <= y <= math.ceil(m['y1']+(box[3]-height)*scale)+1
                            for m in masks) for y in range(y0,y1) for x in range(x0,x1))
                        if dark: continue
                        reason = 'white DOI link beneath a later painted identical DOI'
                    else:
                        # The margin of a white glyph can reach the ink of the painted
                        # glyph set right after it (AG05: "***" painted white before a
                        # printed "*"). Pixels inside a neighbouring painted glyph's own
                        # rectangle are that glyph's; the white glyph needs at least
                        # half its own rectangle tested and white there.
                        near = [(math.floor(o['x0']-box[0]*scale)-1, math.floor(o['y0']+(box[3]-height)*scale)-1,
                                 math.ceil(o['x1']-box[0]*scale)+1, math.ceil(o['y1']+(box[3]-height)*scale)+1)
                                for j, o in enumerate(cs_all) if j != i and (e['id'], j) not in candidate
                                and o['x0'] < c['x1']+2 and o['x1'] > c['x0']-2 and o['y0'] < c['y1']+2 and o['y1'] > c['y0']-2]
                        cells = [(x, y) for y in range(y0, y1) for x in range(x0, x1)
                                 if not any(a <= x < c2 and b <= y < d for a, b, c2, d in near)]
                        if not near:
                            if any(min(data[(y*w+x0)*3:(y*w+x1)*3], default=0) < 250 for y in range(y0, y1)): continue
                        elif (len(cells) < .5*(x1-x0)*(y1-y0) or
                              any(min(data[(y*w+x)*3:(y*w+x)*3+3]) < 250 for x, y in cells)): continue
                if reason: reasons[reason].append(i)
            cs = e['_chars']
            if (e['text'].startswith(('•�', '●�', '◦�')) and len(cs) > 2 and
                    cs[1]['text'] == '�' and cs[1].get('dropped_control') and
                    cs[0]['font'] == cs[1]['font'] and
                    cs[1]['y1']-cs[1]['y0'] < .01*cs[1]['size']):
                reasons['empty glyph spacer after a printed bullet'].append(1)
            if reasons:
                omit_run_characters(p, e, sorted({i for ids in reasons.values() for i in ids}), '; '.join(reasons),
                                    overlay=any(r.startswith('white DOI') for r in reasons))
        omit_rotated_table_watermarks(p)


def front_link_underlays(runs, candidate):
    """Identify duplicate white URI characters by paint order, value and baseline.

    Merely overlapping text, a different DOI, a later white layer, or a visible
    URI supplies no mask. Raster evidence must still establish white elsewhere.
    """
    masks = {}
    for e in runs:
        groups = collections.defaultdict(list)
        for i, c in enumerate(e['_chars']):
            if c.get('paint_order'): groups[c['paint_order'][0]].append((i,c))
        texts = {order: ''.join(c['text'] for i,c in sorted(cs,key=lambda pair:pair[1]['paint_order']))
                 for order,cs in groups.items()}
        for order, cs in groups.items():
            link = re.fullmatch(r'https?://(?:dx\.)?doi\.org/(10\.\d{4,9}/\S+)', texts[order], re.I)
            if not link or not all(candidate.get((e['id'],i),'').startswith('white') for i,c in cs): continue
            for later, visible in groups.items():
                if later <= order or any((e['id'],i) in candidate for i,c in visible): continue
                doi = re.fullmatch(r'(?:DOI:|https?://(?:dx\.)?doi\.org/)?(10\.\d{4,9}/\S+)', texts[later], re.I)
                if not doi or doi[1] != link[1]: continue
                if abs(statistics.median(c['origin'][1] for i,c in cs)-
                       statistics.median(c['origin'][1] for i,c in visible)) > .25*e['run_size_px']: continue
                for i,c in cs: masks[e['id'],i] = [c for j,c in visible]
    return masks


def omit_rotated_table_watermarks(page):
    """Apply rule 1's diagonal proof-layer witness in a sideways table frame.

    Older boxer records deliberately deferred these overlays to the consumer.
    Require the same large, light/outline, collinear eight-letter overlay spanning
    both page axes. Only a table in the overlay's snapped reading frame enables
    removal. Keep native character indices and omission evidence; the gate can
    then rebuild baselines that the oversized letters distorted.
    """
    import math
    chars = table_characters(page)
    angles = {round(t.get('angle', 0)) % 360 for t in page.get('tables', [])} & {90, 270}
    if not chars or not angles: return
    size = sorted(c['size'] for c in chars)[len(chars)//2]
    groups = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for i, c in enumerate(chars):
        color = c.get('fill_color')
        light = (isinstance(color, (int, float)) and color >= .65 or
                 isinstance(color, (list, tuple)) and
                 (len(color) == 3 and all(isinstance(v, (int, float)) and v >= .65 for v in color) or
                  len(color) == 4 and all(isinstance(v, (int, float)) for v in color) and sum(color) <= .35))
        snapped = min((0, 90, 270, 360), key=lambda a: abs(a-c['angle'])) % 360
        if (snapped in angles and c['size'] >= 3*size
                and min(abs(c['angle']-a) for a in (0, 90, 180, 270, 360)) >= 15
                and (light or c.get('render_mode') in (1, 2, 5, 6) or c.get('fill_alpha', 1) <= .5)):
            groups[snapped, c['angle'], c.get('font'), round(c['size'])].append((i, c))
    removed = set()
    box = page['region_box_px']
    for (angle, *_), group in groups.items():
        if sum(c['text'].isalpha() for i, c in group) < 8: continue
        a = min(group, key=lambda pair: pair[1]['origin'][0])[1]
        z = max(group, key=lambda pair: pair[1]['origin'][0])[1]
        dx, dy = z['origin'][0]-a['origin'][0], z['origin'][1]-a['origin'][1]
        if abs(dx) < .4*(box[2]-box[0]) or abs(dy) < .4*(box[3]-box[1]): continue
        if any(abs(dx*(c['origin'][1]-a['origin'][1])-dy*(c['origin'][0]-a['origin'][0]))
               > .2*size*math.hypot(dx, dy) for i, c in group): continue
        removed.update(i for i, c in group)
        page.setdefault('_table_native_angles', set()).add(angle)
    for e in page['elements']:
        indices = [j for j, i in enumerate(e.get('char_ids', [])) if i in removed]
        if indices and e.get('_chars'):
            omit_run_characters(page, e, indices, 'large diagonal watermark crossing the page body', overlay=True)


def restore_citation_ownership(pages):
    """Undo an interleaved numeric superscript using verified native adjacency.

    Both runs must belong to prose outside tables/floats. The digits are smaller
    than their current line, consecutive in the source character stream, and
    immediately follow the last character of a different run. They must also
    sit just to its right and above its baseline. Original spaces and letters
    survive; no numeric text or dash is inferred from the saved garbled string.
    """
    for p in pages:
        p.pop('copy_citation_moves', None)
        blocks = {b['id']: b for b in p.get('blocks', [])}
        allowed = {m for b in (p.get('main_text') or {}).get('blocks', []) if b['cls']=='main'
                   for m in blocks.get(b['id'], {}).get('members', [])}
        protected = {m for t in p.get('tables', []) for m in t['members']}
        protected |= {m for f in p.get('floats', []) for bid in f['blocks'] for m in blocks.get(bid, {}).get('members', [])}
        rs = [e for e in p['elements'] if e['kind']=='text' and e['id'] in allowed-protected and
              e.get('_chars') and len(e['_chars'])==len(e.get('char_ids',[])) and e['angle']==0]
        owners = {cid:e for e in rs for cid in e['char_ids']}
        moves = []
        for e in rs:
            size = e.get('run_size_px', 0)
            small = [(cid,c) for cid,c in zip(e['char_ids'], e['_chars']) if c['size'] < .82*size]
            groups = []
            for cid,c in sorted(small):
                if groups and cid==groups[-1][-1][0]+1: groups[-1].append((cid,c))
                else: groups.append([(cid,c)])
            for group in groups:
                text = ''.join(c['text'] for cid,c in group)
                if not re.fullmatch(r'\d+(?:[,–-]\d+)*',text): continue
                target = owners.get(group[0][0]-1)
                if target is None or target is e or target['char_ids'][-1]!=group[0][0]-1: continue
                prev = target['_chars'][-1]; ts = target.get('run_size_px',0)
                mark = group[0][1]
                if (not re.search(r'[A-Za-z]{3}',target['text']) or not prev['text'].strip() or
                    not all(c['size'] < .82*ts and .15*ts < prev['origin'][1]-c['origin'][1] < .7*ts for _,c in group) or
                    not -.05*ts <= mark['origin'][0]-(prev.get('adv_x1') or prev['x1']) <= .5*ts): continue
                moves.append((e,target,group))
        for source,target,group in moves:
            ids = {cid for cid,c in group}; positions = []; offset = 0
            for cid,c in zip(source['char_ids'],source['_chars']):
                # source_typography already verified all nonspace letters.
                for letter in c['text']:
                    if letter.isspace(): continue
                    while offset < len(source['text']) and source['text'][offset].isspace(): offset += 1
                    if cid in ids: positions.append(offset)
                    offset += 1
            before = source['text']; selected = set(positions)
            source['text'] = ''.join(ch for i,ch in enumerate(before) if i not in selected)
            kept = [(cid,c) for cid,c in zip(source['char_ids'],source['_chars']) if cid not in ids]
            source['char_ids'] = [cid for cid,c in kept]; source['_chars'] = [c for cid,c in kept]
            target['text'] += ''.join(c['text'] for cid,c in group)
            target['char_ids'].extend(cid for cid,c in group); target['_chars'].extend(c for cid,c in group)
            for e in (source,target):
                e['box_px'] = hull([c['x0'],c['y0'],c['x1'],c['y1']] for c in e['_chars'])
            p.setdefault('copy_citation_moves',[]).append(dict(source=source['id'],target=target['id'],
                char_ids=sorted(ids),text=''.join(c['text'] for _,c in group),before=before,after=source['text']))


def raised_power_spans(text, chars):
    """Spell a verified small, raised digit sequence after base 10 as 10^n.

    Keep saved spacing everywhere else. Require an exact character witness,
    two full-size baseline digits and adjacent smaller digits raised above
    them. Ordinary baseline numbers and superscript citations after words
    retain their existing rendering. A digit must have digit-height ink: a
    symbol font may encode a minus bar as a numeral. Such malformed readings
    remain unchanged rather than becoming a false positive exponent. This
    also covers detached exponent runs.
    """
    glyphs=[(letter,c) for c in chars for letter in c['text'] if not letter.isspace()]
    positions=[i for i,ch in enumerate(text) if not ch.isspace()]
    if ''.join(ch for ch,c in glyphs)!=''.join(text[i] for i in positions):return []
    evidence={i:c for i,(_,c) in zip(positions,glyphs)}; edits=[]
    for m in re.finditer(r'(?:(?<![\w.])(?:[×xX]\s*)?|(?<=\d[xX]))(10)\s*([−+–-]?\d+)',text):
        a=evidence[m.start(1)]; b=evidence[m.start(1)+1]
        exp=[evidence[i] for i in range(m.start(2),m.end(2))]
        if not all('size' in c and 'origin' in c for c in [a,b,*exp]):continue
        em=b['size']
        if any(text[i].isdigit() and 'y0' in evidence[i] and
               evidence[i]['y1']-evidence[i]['y0'] < .35*evidence[i]['size']
               for i in range(m.start(2),m.end(2))):continue
        if (abs(a['size']-em)>.1*em or abs(a['origin'][1]-b['origin'][1])>.1*em or
            not all(c['size']<.82*em and .15*em<b['origin'][1]-c['origin'][1]<.7*em for c in exp) or
            not -.1*em<=exp[0]['origin'][0]-(b.get('adv_x1') or b['x1'])<=.5*em):continue
        edits.append((m.end(1),m.start(2)))
    return edits


def raised_power_text(text, chars):
    """Keep the established caret rendering for verified powers of ten."""
    for start,end in reversed(raised_power_spans(text,chars)):text=text[:start]+'^'+text[end:]
    return text


def citation_numbers(pages):
    """Numbering witnessed by reference rows, including separately printed labels.

    Only reference-classified blocks supply rows. A detached label must share a
    row's baseline immediately to its left; consecutive numbers corroborate the
    list. A lone numeric continuation or a publication year cannot establish it.
    """
    numbers = set()
    for p in pages:
        refs = {b['id'] for b in (p.get('main_text') or {}).get('blocks', []) if b.get('in_refs')}
        ids = {m for b in p.get('blocks', []) if b['id'] in refs for m in b['members']}
        runs = [e for e in p['elements'] if e['kind']=='text']
        labels = [e for e in runs if re.fullmatch(r'\s*(?:\d{1,3}\.|\[\d{1,3}\])\s*', e['text'])]
        for e in runs:
            if e['id'] in ids and REF_NUMBER.match(e['text']):
                numbers.add(int(re.search(r'\d+',e['text'])[0]))
        for row in text_rows([e for e in runs if e['id'] in ids]):
            text = ' '.join(e['text'].strip() for e in row)
            if REF_NUMBER.match(text): numbers.add(int(re.search(r'\d+', text)[0]))
            for first in row:
                box = first['box_px']; em = first.get('run_size_px', 16)
                for label in labels:
                    lb = label['box_px']
                    if 0 <= box[0]-lb[2] <= 3*em and abs(lb[3]-box[3]) < .3*em:
                        numbers.add(int(re.search(r'\d+', label['text'])[0]))
    return {n for n in numbers if n-1 in numbers or n+1 in numbers}


def prepare_front_mark_labels(pages, authors):
    """Existing byline/address ownership identifies affiliation labels, not citations.

    This only annotates native marks; front-matter placement and text are left
    to their existing rules. In particular, a raised address prefix after a
    semicolon must not turn into a bibliography citation during prose reading.
    """
    if not pages:return
    page=pages[front_page_index(pages,authors)]
    owned=set(authors.get('author_run_ids',[]))|set(authors.get('title_run_ids',[]))
    box=authors.get('affiliation_box_px')
    for e in page['elements']:
        if e['kind']!='text':continue
        b=e['box_px'];em=e.get('run_size_px',16)
        address=box and box[0]-em<=b[0] and b[2]<=box[2]+em and box[1]-.5*em<=b[1] and b[3]<=box[3]+1
        if e['id'] in owned or address:
            for c in e.get('_chars',[]):c['_mark_not_citation']=True


def prepare_wrapped_citations(pages, numbers):
    """A citation may wrap between digits; retain that one group across rows.

    Require consecutive native characters in one text block, matching small
    type at both line edges, ordinary line leading and witnessed combined
    numbering. No baseline digit is moved into or out of the citation.
    """
    for p in pages:
        runs = {e['id']:e for e in p['elements'] if e['kind']=='text' and e.get('_chars')}
        for block in p.get('blocks', []):
            rs = [runs[m] for m in block['members'] if m in runs]
            owners = {cid:e for e in rs for cid in e.get('char_ids', [])}
            for prev in rs:
                cs=prev['_chars']; ids=prev.get('char_ids',[])
                if not ids or len(ids)!=len(cs) or prev.get('angle',0):continue
                base=max(cs,key=lambda c:c['size']); em=base['size'];tail=[]
                for c in reversed(cs):
                    if c['size']<.82*em and .15*em<base['origin'][1]-c['origin'][1]<.7*em:tail.insert(0,c)
                    else:break
                if not tail:continue
                parts=[tail]; edges=[]; current=prev
                # The middle of a long reference group can occupy entire rows
                # of small type. Follow exact native character succession until
                # ordinary baseline text resumes, then validate the whole group.
                while True:
                    following=owners.get(current['char_ids'][-1]+1)
                    if following is None or following is current or following.get('angle',0):break
                    fc=following['_chars'];fi=following.get('char_ids',[])
                    if not fi or fi[0]!=current['char_ids'][-1]+1 or len(fi)!=len(fc):break
                    leading=[]
                    for c in fc:
                        if c['size']<.82*em and abs(c['size']-tail[0]['size'])<.1*em:leading.append(c)
                        else:break
                    if (not leading or not .7*em<leading[0]['origin'][1]-parts[-1][-1]['origin'][1]<2.3*em
                            or leading[0]['x0']>=parts[-1][-1]['x0']-3*em):break
                    if len(leading)<len(fc) and not .15*em<fc[len(leading)]['origin'][1]-leading[0]['origin'][1]<.7*em:break
                    edges.append((parts[-1][-1],leading[0]));parts.append(leading);current=following
                    if len(leading)<len(fc):break
                mark=''.join(c['text'] for part in parts for c in part).replace(' ','')
                if (not edges or not re.fullmatch(r'\d+(?:[,–-]\d+)*',mark)
                        or not all(int(n) in numbers for n in re.findall(r'\d+',mark))):continue
                for last,first in edges:
                    last['_citation_continues']=True;first['_citation_continued']=True
                text=prev.get('text',''.join(c['text'] for c in cs))
                rendered=raised_mark_text(text,cs,numbers)
                # Never emit an orphan closing bracket when the starting mark
                # fails the ordinary unit, variable, spacing or glyph checks.
                if rendered.count('[')<=text.count('['):
                    for last,first in edges:
                        last.pop('_citation_continues',None);first.pop('_citation_continued',None)


def raised_mark_text(text, chars, numbers, table=False, citations_only=False):
    """Render native raised marks without changing any source letter or digit.

    Exact glyph agreement, smaller type and a raised baseline are mandatory.
    Citation syntax alone is insufficient: every cited number must be witnessed
    in the bibliography. Scientific units and charges are not citations. A
    following baseline numeral is separated from the closing citation bracket.
    """
    glyphs = [(ch,c) for c in chars for ch in c['text'] if not ch.isspace()]
    positions = [i for i,ch in enumerate(text) if not ch.isspace()]
    if ''.join(ch for ch,c in glyphs) != ''.join(text[i] for i in positions): return text
    if not glyphs or any('size' not in c or 'origin' not in c for _,c in glyphs): return text
    # Preserve the established exponent rule, including its malformed-glyph veto.
    power_starts = {start for start,end in raised_power_spans(text,chars)}
    edits = []; i = 0 if glyphs[0][1].get('_citation_continued') else 1
    while i < len(glyphs):
        ch, first = glyphs[i]
        continued = bool(first.get('_citation_continued'))
        prev, base = glyphs[i-1] if i else ('',max((c for _,c in glyphs),key=lambda c:c['size']))
        whole_continuation=continued and all(c['size']<=first['size']*1.1 for _,c in glyphs)
        em = base['size']
        def raised(c):
            if whole_continuation:
                return abs(c['size']-first['size'])<.1*em and abs(c['origin'][1]-first['origin'][1])<.1*em
            return c['size'] < .82*em and .15*em < base['origin'][1]-c['origin'][1] < .7*em
        if (not raised(first) or not continued and not -.1*em <= first['origin'][0]-(base.get('adv_x1') or base['x1']) <= .5*em):
            i += 1; continue
        end = i+1
        while end < len(glyphs) and raised(glyphs[end][1]): end += 1
        mark = ''.join(ch for ch,c in glyphs[i:end])
        start, stop = positions[i], positions[end-1]+1
        prefix = text[:positions[i-1]+1] if i else ''
        unit = re.search(r'(?<![A-Za-z])(?:[µμnmcdkK]?[mM]|kg|[µμm]?s|mol|[kMGT]?Hz|Pa|ft|in|[kMGT]?bp)\)?$', prefix)
        symbol = re.search(r'(?<![\w-])([^\W\d_]{1,2})$',prefix)
        variable = (symbol and (len(symbol[1])==1 or not base.get('_body_font',False))
                    and not re.search(r'(?<!\w)et\s+al$',prefix,re.I))
        # A raised digit after a closing mathematical expression is a power,
        # even when the bibliography happens to contain that digit. Fragmented
        # equation rows may retain only their function name and parentheses.
        parenthesis = re.search(r'\(([^()]*)\)$',prefix)
        publication_year = (parenthesis and re.fullmatch(r'(?:18|19|20)\d{2}[a-z]?',parenthesis[1])
                            and (table or re.search(r'(?:\bet al\.?|\b[A-Z][a-zÀ-ž]+)\s*$',prefix[:parenthesis.start()])))
        math_expression = prev in ')]}' and (re.search(
            r'[=∑∏√×÷^]|(?<!\w)(?:ln|log|sin|cos|tan|exp)(?!\w)',prefix)
            or parenthesis and not re.search(r'[^\W\d_]{3}',parenthesis[1])
            and not publication_year and not re.fullmatch(r'\s*\d+(?:[.,]\d+)?\s*%\s*',parenthesis[1]))
        # A radical dot may share the citation's raised baseline. Its symbol
        # stays with the chemical formula; only the following digits are cited.
        radical = mark[:1] if mark.startswith(('•','·')) else ''
        citation_mark = mark[len(radical):]
        # Prefix isotope numbers attach to the following element, not the
        # preceding prose. Mathematical powers also occur on short variables
        # immediately before their function arguments.
        isotope = (mark.isdigit() and start>positions[i-1]+1 and
                   bool(re.match(r'[A-Z][a-z]?(?:[-−–\s,.;)]|$)',text[stop:]))) if i else False
        variable_argument = (mark.isdigit() and text[stop:].startswith('(')
                             and bool(re.search(r'(?<!\w)[^\W\d_]{1,2}$',prefix)))
        continues=bool(glyphs[end-1][1].get('_citation_continues'))
        citation = bool(numbers and not unit and re.fullmatch(r'[\d,–-]+' if continues or continued else r'\d+(?:[,–-]\d+)*', mark)
                        and (glyphs[end-1][1].get('_citation_continues') or continued or
                             all(int(n) in numbers for n in re.findall(r'\d+', mark))))
        if radical and re.fullmatch(r'\d+(?:[,–-]\d+)*',citation_mark):
            citation=bool(numbers and all(int(n) in numbers for n in re.findall(r'\d+',citation_mark)))
        if (math_expression or isotope or variable_argument or variable and not radical
                or not continued and not (prev.isalnum() or prev in '.,;:)]}’”\"\'')):citation=False
        if any(c.get('_mark_not_citation') for ch,c in glyphs[i:end]):citation=False
        # A genuine power of ten takes precedence over an otherwise possible citation.
        is_power = bool(i and positions[i-1]+1 in power_starts)
        # Existing font checks may retain a rejected mapping to protect layout.
        # Do not give that unresolved glyph a new mathematical interpretation.
        if not is_power and any(c.get('_mark_unverified') for ch,c in glyphs[i:end]):
            i=end;continue
        if citations_only and not (is_power or citation or continued):
            i=end;continue
        if is_power: replacement = '^'+mark
        elif citation or continued:
            replacement = radical+('' if continued else '[')+citation_mark+('' if glyphs[end-1][1].get('_citation_continues') else ']')
        elif prev.isdigit() and not isotope and re.fullmatch(r'[\w−+–*/′″\x27’.-]+',mark): replacement = '^'+mark
        else:
            i = end; continue
        # Do not reinterpret digit-shaped encodings of a minus bar.
        if any(ch.isdigit() and 'y0' in c and c['y1']-c['y0'] < .35*c['size'] for ch,c in glyphs[i:end]):
            i = end; continue
        start = positions[i-1]+1 if i else start
        if citation and stop < len(text) and text[stop].isalnum(): replacement += ' '
        edits.append((start,stop,replacement)); i = end
    for start,stop,replacement in reversed(edits): text = text[:start]+replacement+text[stop:]
    return text


def raised_mark_placed(run, page, placed, index_type):
    """A placed marked row accounts for its original unmarked source runs.

    Both sides must be present: the run in this page's recorded native row,
    and that row's marked text in the actual copy. This prevents a numeric
    token split by brackets from becoming a false missing-text warning.
    """
    return any(run['id'] in mark['runs'] and index_type(mark['before']).contains(run['text'])
               and placed.contains(mark['after']) for mark in page.get('_raised_marks',[]))


def prose_row(row, p, render_marks=True):
    """Use x positions to insert detached citations inside a run; retain ordinary saved word spacing.
    Report emphasis only when almost the whole row uses a non-body bold/italic/small-caps face."""
    row=lowered_digit_runs(row)
    f=frame(row[0]['angle'])
    if any(e.get('_superscript') for e in row):
        words=sorted(table_words(row,p,f), key=lambda t:t[0][0]); text=''
        for b,t,e in words: text += ('' if not text or e.get('_superscript') else ' ') + t
    else:
        text=''
        for i,e in enumerate(row):
            sep=' ' if text else ''
            if i and row[i-1].get('_chars') and e.get('_chars'):
                a,b=row[i-1]['_chars'][-1],e['_chars'][0]
                if (round(e['angle'])%360==0 and (len(row[i-1]['text'])<=12 or row[i-1].get('_drop_cap_row')) and
                    re.search(r'[a-z]$',text) and re.match(r'[a-z]',e['text']) and a['font']==b['font'] and
                    b['x0']-(a.get('adv_x1') or a['x1'])<(.18 if row[i-1].get('_drop_cap_row') else .08)*min(a['size'],b['size'])): sep=''
            text+=sep+e['text'].strip()
    ordered=positioned_prose_text(row,p)
    if ordered is not None:text=ordered
    if all(round(e['angle'])%360==0 and e.get('_chars') for e in row):
        cs=sorted((c for e in row for c in e['_chars']),key=lambda c:c['x0'])
        before=text
        text=raised_mark_text(text,cs,p['_citation_numbers']) if render_marks and '_citation_numbers' in p else raised_power_text(text,cs)
        if text!=before and re.search(r"[.?!]['\")\]’”]*\d+(?:[,–-]\d+)*$",before) and re.search(r'\[\d+(?:[,–-]\d+)*\]$',text):
            _RAISED_SENTENCE_ENDINGS.add(re.sub(r'[\s\-‐‑–]','',text)[-64:])
        if render_marks and text!=before and '_raised_marks' in p:
            record=dict(runs=[e.get('id') for e in row],box=hull(e['box_px'] for e in row),before=before,after=text)
            if record not in p['_raised_marks']:p['_raised_marks'].append(record)
    if any(e.get('_subscript_sources') for e in row):
        sources=[s for e in row for s in e.get('_subscript_sources', [e])]
        record=dict(runs=[e.get('id') for e in sources],
            before=' '.join(e['text'].strip() for e in sources), after=text,
            why='native lowered digits joined to their unique adjacent formula character')
        evidence=p.setdefault('copy_line_order', [])
        if record not in evidence: evidence.append(record)
    counts=collections.Counter()
    for e in row: counts.update(e.get('_faces',{}))
    emphasis = bool(counts) and sum(n for face,n in counts.items() if styled_face(face,p.get('_body_face',''))) >= .9*sum(counts.values())
    return trace_row(p.get('page'), row, text), emphasis


def mark_reading_frame(chars, angle):
    """Express native glyph boxes, baselines and advances in the text's frame.

    The superscript tests measure along and above a baseline, not necessarily
    the page's x/y axes. Keep the original witnesses unmodified; incomplete
    native coordinates cannot establish a raised mark.
    """
    if not round(angle)%360:return chars
    f=frame(angle);result=[]
    for c in chars:
        if (not all(isinstance(c.get(k),(int,float)) for k in ('x0','y0','x1','y1','adv_x1','adv_y1'))
                or not isinstance(c.get('origin'),(list,tuple)) or len(c['origin'])!=2
                or not all(isinstance(v,(int,float)) for v in c['origin'])):return []
        x0,y0,x1,y1=f([c['x0'],c['y0'],c['x1'],c['y1']])
        x,y=c['origin'];origin=f([x,y,x,y])[:2]
        ax,ay=c['adv_x1'],c['adv_y1'];advance=f([ax,ay,ax,ay])[:2]
        result.append(dict(c,x0=x0,y0=y0,x1=x1,y1=y1,origin=origin,
                           adv_x1=advance[0],adv_y1=advance[1],angle=0))
    return result


def positioned_prose_text(row, page):
    """Interleave separately painted pieces only on a verified ordinary baseline.

    Font layers can leave a ligature, bold number or dash behind a run spanning
    its position. Require readable glyphs, exact native-character alignment, near-equal sizes
    and baselines, and no genuine overlapping ink. Existing spaces stand for
    adjacent characters of one run; an inserted piece uses glyph advances at
    its two new boundaries. Order by placement origins: an italic overhang
    cannot precede the punctuation its origin follows. Stacked equations and
    overprinted text abstain. A row needs a word or a plain numeric citation
    range; mathematical operators and slash-delimited identifiers disqualify
    it even on a shared baseline. Letter ink must also reach the ordinary
    baseline, so a raised sign mislabelled as a letter cannot enter a word.
    """
    if len(row)<2 or any(round(e.get('angle',0))%360 or e.get('_superscript') for e in row):return None
    import unicodedata
    saved=' '.join(e['text'] for e in row)
    if any(unicodedata.category(c)=='Sm' or c in '^_/' for c in saved):return None
    citation_range=(re.fullmatch(r'[\d\s.:;,()–—-]+',saved) and ':' in saved and
                    any(c in saved for c in '–—-'))
    if not re.search(r'[^\W\d_]{3,}',saved) and not citation_range:return None
    glyphs=[]
    for ri,e in enumerate(row):
        cs=e.get('_chars',[]);positions=[i for i,ch in enumerate(e['text']) if not ch.isspace()]
        # An unreadable code (the reader's dropped control) has no trustworthy glyph place; a glyph the
        # glyph checks flagged as U+FFFD keeps its native box and origin, so it is ordered like its native
        # character (lane claims R1: AP22's cedilla glyph claimed as 'x' stays inside its word, flagged).
        if '�' in e['text'] and (len(cs)!=len(positions) or
                                 any(c.get('text')=='�' and (c.get('dropped_control') or not c.get('source_code')) for c in cs)):return None
        if not cs or len(cs)!=len(positions) or ''.join(c['text'] for c in cs)!=re.sub(r'\s','',e['text']):return None
        for i,c in enumerate(cs):
            if any(k not in c for k in ('origin','size','x0','x1','y0','y1')):return None
            space=bool(i and e['text'][positions[i-1]+1:positions[i]])
            glyphs.append((c,ri,i,space))
    size=statistics.median(c['size'] for c,_,_,_ in glyphs)
    baseline=statistics.median(c['origin'][1] for c,_,_,_ in glyphs)
    if any(abs(c['size']-size)>.12*size or abs(c['origin'][1]-baseline)>.08*size for c,_,_,_ in glyphs):return None
    letters=[c for c,_,_,_ in glyphs if c['text'].isalpha()]
    if len(letters)>=3:
        bottom=statistics.median(c['y1'] for c in letters)
        if any(abs(c['y1']-bottom)>.25*size for c in letters):return None
    ordered=sorted(glyphs,key=lambda item:item[0]['origin'][0])
    if [(ri,i) for c,ri,i,s in ordered]==[(ri,i) for c,ri,i,s in glyphs]:return None
    ink_order=sorted(glyphs,key=lambda item:item[0]['x0'])
    for j,(c,ri,i,space) in enumerate(ink_order):
        for d,rj,_,_ in ink_order[j+1:]:
            if d['x0']>=c['x1']:break
            if ri==rj:continue
            overlap=min(c['x1'],d['x1'])-max(c['x0'],d['x0'])
            vertical=min(c['y1'],d['y1'])-max(c['y0'],d['y0'])
            if overlap>.5*max(c['x1']-c['x0'],d['x1']-d['x0']) and vertical>.5*min(c['y1']-c['y0'],d['y1']-d['y0']):return None
    text='';previous=None
    for c,ri,i,space in ordered:
        if previous is not None:
            d,rj,j,_=previous
            if ri!=rj or i!=j+1:
                space=c['origin'][0]-(d.get('adv_x1') or d['x1'])>.16*min(c['size'],d['size'])
            if space:text+=' '
        text+=c['text'];previous=(c,ri,i,space)
    record=dict(runs=[e.get('id') for e in row],
        before=' '.join(e['text'].strip() for e in row),after=text,
        why='native glyphs on one baseline; separated font pieces restored to x order')
    evidence=page.setdefault('copy_line_order',[])
    if record not in evidence:evidence.append(record)
    return text


def positioned_run_placed(run, page, index):
    """A reordered source run is accounted for only by its complete replacement text."""
    return any(run['id'] in record['runs'] and index.contains(record['after'])
               for record in page.get('copy_line_order',[]))


def prepare_table_mark_notes(items, pages):
    """A numbered table note is not a bibliography citation.

    Require a small raised numeric prefix before a table-owned note's words.
    Share those labels across continuations of that same
    numbered table; never change the table's layout or source ownership.
    """
    page_by_number={p['page']:p for p in pages}
    notes=collections.defaultdict(set); tables=[]
    for item in items:
        if item['kind']!='table':continue
        label=re.match(r'^(?:(?:Supplemental|Supplementary)\s+)?Table\s+(S?'+TABLE_NUMBER+r')\b',item.get('caption',''),re.I)
        key=label[0].casefold() if label else (item['page'],item.get('id'))
        tables.append((item,key))
        ids={w['id'] for line in item.get('table_source',{}).get('lines',[]) for w in line['items']}
        for e in page_by_number[item['page']]['elements']:
            if e['id'] not in ids:continue
            match=re.match(r'^\s*(\d{1,3})[.)]?\s*[^\W\d_]',e.get('text',''))
            cs=[c for c in mark_reading_frame(e.get('_chars',[]),e.get('angle',0)) if c['text'].strip()]
            base=next((c for c in cs if c['text'].isalpha()),None)
            if not match or base is None or len(cs)<=len(match[1]):continue
            em=base['size']
            if all(c['size']<.82*em and .15*em<base['origin'][1]-c['origin'][1]<.7*em for c in cs[:len(match[1])]):
                notes[key].add(int(match[1]))
    for item,key in tables:item['_mark_note_numbers']=notes[key]


def table_raised_marks(lines, item, page):
    """Apply glyph-witnessed marks only after the table's layout decision.

    The table source bounds the replacement vocabulary. Repeated source words
    must agree on typography; an ambiguous word is left alone. Glyph geometry
    is measured in the table's reading frame. This preserves
    grid construction, source checks, values and ownership exactly. Newly
    supported rotated and detached runs also retain verified powers of ten;
    other scientific and table-note notation is preserved.
    """
    ids={w['id'] for line in item.get('table_source',{}).get('lines',[]) for w in line['items']}
    ids.update(item.get('table_source',{}).get('caption_ids',[]))
    candidates=collections.defaultdict(set); evidence={}; witnesses=[]
    for e in page['elements']:
        if e['id'] not in ids or not e.get('_chars'):continue
        cs=[dict(c,text=ch) for c in mark_reading_frame(e['_chars'],e.get('angle',0))
            for ch in c['text'] if not ch.isspace()]
        if ''.join(c['text'] for c in cs)!=re.sub(r'\s','',e['text']):continue
        witnesses.append((e,cs))
    # A raised group can have its own native run. Combine only adjacent
    # baseline/mark witnesses; the shared renderer still checks size, rise,
    # bibliography numbering and scientific notation. Do not change cells.
    joined=[]
    for e,cs in witnesses:
        if not re.fullmatch(r'[−+–-]?\d+(?:[,–-]\d+)*',e['text'].strip()):continue
        for prev,pcs in witnesses:
            if prev is e or round(prev.get('angle',0))%360!=round(e.get('angle',0))%360:continue
            a,b=pcs[-1],cs[0];em=a.get('size',0)
            if (not em or not all('origin' in c for c in (a,b))
                    or not b['size']<.82*em or not .15*em<a['origin'][1]-b['origin'][1]<.7*em
                    or not -.1*em<=b['origin'][0]-(a.get('adv_x1') or a['x1'])<=.5*em):continue
            # Both spaced and touching forms occur in table assembly.
            for sep in ('',' '):
                joined.append((dict(prev,text=prev['text'].rstrip()+sep+e['text'].strip(),
                                   _mark_runs=[prev['id'],e['id']],box_px=hull([prev['box_px'],e['box_px']])),pcs+cs))
    for e,cs in witnesses+joined:
        pieces=[(e['text'],cs)];offset=0
        for m in re.finditer(r'\S+',e['text']):
            end=offset+len(m[0]);pieces.append((m[0],cs[offset:end]));offset=end
        for text,chars in pieces:
            # Token matching must not discard an equation's surrounding
            # context and turn its parenthesized integer into a cited year.
            year_context=not re.search(r'[=∑∏√×÷^]|(?<!\w)(?:ln|log|sin|cos|tan|exp)(?!\w)',e['text'])
            marked=raised_mark_text(text,chars,page.get('_citation_numbers',set())-item.get('_mark_note_numbers',set()),
                                   table=year_context,citations_only=bool(round(e.get('angle',0))%360 or e.get('_mark_runs')))
            candidates[text].add(marked)
            if marked!=text:evidence[text]=dict(runs=e.get('_mark_runs',[e['id']]),box=e['box_px'],before=text,after=marked)
    replacements={text:next(iter(values)) for text,values in candidates.items() if len(values)==1 and text not in values}
    if not replacements:return lines
    pattern=re.compile(r'(?<!\w)(?:'+'|'.join(re.escape(s) for s in sorted(replacements,key=len,reverse=True))+r')(?!\w)')
    def replace(m):
        record=evidence[m[0]]
        if record not in page['_raised_marks']:page['_raised_marks'].append(record)
        return replacements[m[0]]
    return [pattern.sub(replace,line) for line in lines]


def emphasis_prefix(row, p, text):
    """Length of a verified leading emphasized phrase, including an emphasized list label."""
    if not all(e.get('_chars') for e in row): return 0
    cs=[c for e in row for c in e['_chars']]
    if re.sub(r'\s','',text)!=re.sub(r'\s','',''.join(c['text'] for c in cs)): return 0
    n=0
    for c in cs:
        if not styled_face(re.sub(r'^[A-Z]{6}\+','',c['font']),p.get('_body_face','')): break
        n+=len(re.sub(r'\s','',c['text']))
    positions=[i for i,ch in enumerate(text) if not ch.isspace()]
    return positions[n-1]+1 if n else 0


def glyph_spacing(e):
    """Repair only spaces contradicted by verified glyph advances, keeping the saved letters intact."""
    cs=e.get('_chars',[]); text=e['text']
    glyphs=[c for c in cs for ch in c['text'] if not ch.isspace()]
    positions=[i for i,ch in enumerate(text) if not ch.isspace()]
    if len(glyphs)!=len(positions) or round(e['angle'])%360: return text
    for k in range(len(positions)-1,0,-1):
        a,b=glyphs[k-1],glyphs[k]; i,j=positions[k-1],positions[k]
        gap=b['x0']-(a.get('adv_x1') or a['x1']); size=min(a['size'],b['size'])
        left,right=text[i],text[j]; between=text[i+1:j]
        if not between and gap>.16*size and (left in '<>≤≥`´' or right in '<>≤≥' or
                left.isdigit() and bool(re.match(r'(?:mm|cm|mg|kg|ml|µm|nm|years?)\b',text[j:])) or left.isalpha() and right=='('):
            text=text[:i+1]+' '+text[j:]
    return text


def round_bullet(c, page):
    """Prove an unmapped bullet from one closed circular outline, never a hollow ring.

    The ink must occupy the middle of a text line. Its decoded name/code is not
    evidence; sample the embedded cubic curves and require a filled round disk.
    """
    reader = sys.modules.get('_copy_table_reader')
    if reader is None or not page.get('_table_pdf'): return False
    w, h = c['x1']-c['x0'], c['y1']-c['y0']
    if not (.2*c['size'] < min(w,h) <= max(w,h) < .55*c['size'] and .9 < w/h < 1.1): return False
    font = next((f for f in reader.sc_doc(page['_table_pdf']).fonts.values() if f.basefont == c['font']), None)
    if not font or not font.prog or font.prog.kind == 'truetype': return False
    gid = font.gmap.get(c.get('source_code'))
    if gid is None or not 0 <= gid < len(font.prog.order): return False
    class Disk:
        def __init__(self): self.points=[]; self.contours=0; self.closed=0; self.valid=True
        def moveTo(self, p): self.contours+=1; self.points.append(p)
        def lineTo(self, p): self.points.append(p)
        def curveTo(self, a, b, z):
            if not self.points: self.valid=False; return
            p=self.points[-1]
            for k in range(1,9):
                t=k/8; s=1-t
                self.points.append(tuple(s**3*p[i]+3*s*s*t*a[i]+3*s*t*t*b[i]+t**3*z[i] for i in (0,1)))
        def closePath(self): self.closed+=1
        def endPath(self): self.valid=False
        def addComponent(self, *args): self.valid=False
    pen=Disk()
    try: font.prog.glyphs[font.prog.order[gid]].draw(pen)
    except (AttributeError, KeyError, TypeError, ValueError): return False
    if not pen.valid or pen.contours != 1 or pen.closed != 1 or len(pen.points)<16: return False
    xs,ys=zip(*pen.points); w=max(xs)-min(xs); h=max(ys)-min(ys)
    if min(w,h)<=0 or not .9<w/h<1.1: return False
    cx,cy=(max(xs)+min(xs))/2,(max(ys)+min(ys))/2
    return all(.85 < ((x-cx)/(w/2))**2+((y-cy)/(h/2))**2 < 1.15 for x,y in pen.points)


def restore_prose_glyphs(pages):
    """Compose spacing accents over their native letter and recover proved list disks.

    Only verified non-float runs are changed. Tables retain their original
    character IDs and text. An accent needs horizontal ink overlap and the
    expected vertical position; ordinary apostrophes cannot qualify. Preserve
    word spacing from the adjacent letters' advances after removing the mark.
    A detached accent joins before row grouping, with both source IDs recorded.
    Unknown disks also need repeated line-leading list positions in the paper.
    """
    import unicodedata
    candidates=[]; bullets=collections.Counter(); checked={}
    for p in pages:
        p.pop('copy_prose_glyphs',None)
        p['copy_line_order']=[]
        frames=[t['box_px'] for t in p.get('tables',[])]+[f['box_px'] for f in p.get('floats',[])]
        for e in p['elements']:
            if e['kind']!='text' or round(e['angle'])%360 or not e.get('_chars'): continue
            if any(x_share(e['box_px'],b)>.5 and min(e['box_px'][3],b[3])>max(e['box_px'][1],b[1]) for b in frames): continue
            cs=e['_chars']; positions=[i for i,ch in enumerate(e['text']) if not ch.isspace()]
            if len(cs)!=len(positions) or any(len(c['text'])!=1 for c in cs): continue
            candidates.append((p,e,positions))
            if cs[0]['text']=='�' and len(cs)>3 and cs[1]['text'].isalpha():
                c=cs[0]; key=(c['font'],c.get('source_code'))
                if key not in checked: checked[key]=round_bullet(c,p)
                if checked[key] and c['y0']>=cs[1]['y0'] and c['y1']<=cs[1]['y1']+.1*c['size']:
                    bullets[key]+=1
    # A separately painted accent must attach before its small ink box can
    # anchor a row. Otherwise lowercase-only words on the true baseline can
    # become another row, and a detached accent can split a wrapped word.
    for p,e,positions in candidates:
        if len(e['_chars'])!=1:continue
        mark=reference_accent_mark(e,p)
        if not mark:continue
        c=e['_chars'][0];targets=[]
        below=all(unicodedata.combining(ch) in (202,216,220) for ch in mark)
        for other_page,other,offsets in candidates:
            if other_page is not p or other is e:continue
            for j,b in enumerate(other['_chars']):
                if not b['text'].isalpha():continue
                overlap=min(c['x1'],b['x1'])-max(c['x0'],b['x0'])
                vertical=(b['y0']<=c['y0']<=b['y1']+.4*b['size'] if below else
                          b['y0']-.6*b['size']<=c['y0'] and c['y1']<b['y1']-.3*b['size'])
                if overlap>0 and vertical:targets.append((overlap,other,j,offsets[j]))
        if not targets:continue
        _,other,j,offset=max(targets,key=lambda x:x[0])
        before=other['text'];base=other['_chars'][j]['text']
        if base=='ı' and not below:base='i'
        composed=unicodedata.normalize('NFC',base+mark)
        if len(composed)!=1:continue
        other['text']=before[:offset]+composed+before[offset+1:]
        other['_chars']=[dict(b,text=composed) if k==j else b for k,b in enumerate(other['_chars'])]
        p.setdefault('copy_prose_glyphs',[]).append(dict(run=other['id'],source_run=e['id'],
            source_char_ids=list(e.get('char_ids',[])),before=before,after=other['text'],
            why='detached accent composed with the native letter under its ink'))
        e['text']='';e['_chars']=[];e['char_ids']=[];e['_accent_consumed']=True
    for p,e,positions in candidates:
        if e.get('_accent_consumed'):continue
        cs=[dict(c) for c in e['_chars']]; changes={}; removed=set()
        for i,c in enumerate(cs):
            key=(c['font'],c.get('source_code'))
            if c['text']=='�' and bullets[key]>=2:
                changes[i]='•'; continue
            mark=SPACING_ACCENT.get(c['text'])
            if not mark and not c['text'].isalnum():
                mark=reference_accent_mark(dict(text=c['text'], _chars=[c],
                    box_px=[c['x0'],c['y0'],c['x1'],c['y1']]),p)
            if not mark: continue
            below=unicodedata.combining(mark) in (202,216,220)
            targets=[]
            for j in range(max(0,i-2),min(len(cs),i+3)):
                b=cs[j]
                if not b['text'].isalpha(): continue
                overlap=min(c['x1'],b['x1'])-max(c['x0'],b['x0'])
                vertical=(b['y0']<=c['y0']<=b['y1']+.4*b['size'] if below else
                          b['y0']-.6*b['size']<=c['y0'] and c['y1']<b['y1']-.3*b['size'])
                if overlap>0 and vertical: targets.append((overlap,j))
            if not targets: continue
            _,j=max(targets)
            base=changes.get(j,cs[j]['text'])
            if base=='ı' and not below: base='i'
            composed=unicodedata.normalize('NFC',base+mark)
            if len(composed)==1: changes[j]=composed; removed.add(i)
        if not changes: continue
        before=e['text']; text=''; kept=[]
        for i,c in enumerate(cs):
            if i in removed: continue
            if kept:
                j=kept[-1]; gap=before[positions[j]+1:positions[i]]
                if any(k in removed for k in range(j+1,i)):
                    advance=cs[j].get('adv_x1') or cs[j]['x1']
                    gap=' ' if c['x0']-advance>.16*min(c['size'],cs[j]['size']) else ''
                text+=gap
            c['text']=changes.get(i,c['text']); text+=c['text']; kept.append(i)
        e['text']=text; e['_chars']=[cs[i] for i in kept]
        e['char_ids']=[e['char_ids'][i] for i in kept]
        p.setdefault('copy_prose_glyphs',[]).append(dict(run=e['id'],before=before,after=text,
            why='overprinted accent or repeated list disk proved by native glyph geometry'))


def table_words(runs, p, f):
    """Smallest trustworthy pieces: the PDF's words, retaining their actual positions and run membership.
    Spaces are recovered from the saved run text, so ligatures/overprint removal cannot invent new words."""
    chars = table_characters(p) if p else []
    out = []
    for e in runs:
        text = e["text"].strip()
        ids = e.get("char_ids", [])
        cs = [chars[i] for i in ids if chars[i]["text"].strip()] if ids and chars and max(ids) < len(chars) else []
        compact = lambda s: re.sub(r"\s", "", s)
        if cs and compact(text) == compact("".join(c["text"] for c in cs)):
            # A ligature may decode to several letters sharing one character box.
            cs = [c for c in cs for ch in c["text"] if not ch.isspace()]
            k = 0
            for m in re.finditer(r"\S+", text):
                word = m.group(); wc = cs[k:k+len(word)]; k += len(word)
                b = f(hull([c['x0'], c['y0'], c['x1'], c['y1']] for c in wc))
                rb = f(e['box_px'])
                # Splitting changes horizontal ownership, never the saved run's baseline.
                out.append(([b[0], rb[1], b[2], rb[3]], word, e))
        else:
            out.append((f(e["box_px"]), text, e))
    return out


def table_lines(items):
    """Group glyph runs/words on baselines; small superscripts belong to the neighbouring baseline."""
    lines = []
    for b, t, e in sorted(items, key=lambda it: ((it[0][1]+it[0][3])/2, it[0][0])):
        vc = (b[1]+b[3])/2; h = max(4., b[3]-b[1])
        if lines and abs(vc-lines[-1]['vc']) <= .5*max(h, lines[-1]['h']):
            L=lines[-1]; L['items'].append((b,t,e)); L['top']=min(L['top'],b[1]); L['bot']=max(L['bot'],b[3])
            if h >= L['h']*.8: L['vc'] = (L['vc']+vc)/2
            L['h']=max(L['h'],h)
        else: lines.append(dict(vc=vc,h=h,top=b[1],bot=b[3],items=[(b,t,e)]))
    for L in lines: L['items'].sort(key=lambda it: it[0][0])
    return lines


def ruled_table_rows(lines, graphics, height):
    """Derive rectangular cells from rules, keeping vertically centred text with its entire row span.
    Only the top header band may repeat across columns; a horizontally merged data cell is emitted once."""
    items=[it for L in lines for it in L['items']]
    u0=min(b[0] for b,t,e in items);u1=max(b[2] for b,t,e in items)
    v0=min(b[1] for b,t,e in items);v1=max(b[3] for b,t,e in items)
    # Cell borders are often separate short strokes, one per printed row. Join touching strokes
    # before testing their length; dropping the short ones invents horizontally spanning data cells.
    vertical=[]
    for b,k in sorted(graphics, key=lambda bk:(round(bk[0][0]),bk[0][1])):
        if k!='rule' or b[2]-b[0]>=.3*height or not u0<b[0]<u1: continue
        if vertical and abs(b[0]-vertical[-1][0])<1 and b[1]<=vertical[-1][3]+1:
            vertical[-1]=hull([vertical[-1],b])
        else: vertical.append(list(b))
    vertical=[b for b in vertical if b[3]-b[1]>3*height]
    if len(vertical)<2:return None
    horizontal=[b for b,k in graphics if k=='rule' and b[3]-b[1]<.3*height and b[2]-b[0]>3*height]
    if len(horizontal)<3:return None
    horizontal += [[b[0],y,b[2],y] for b,k in graphics if k=='fill' for y in (b[1],b[3])]
    def cluster(values):
        result=[]
        for x in sorted(values):
            if not result or x-result[-1]>2: result.append(x)
        return result
    xs=cluster([u0-1,u1+1]+[(b[0]+b[2])/2 for b in vertical])
    ys=cluster([min([v0-1]+[b[1] for b in horizontal]),max([v1+1]+[b[3] for b in horizontal])]+[(b[1]+b[3])/2 for b in horizontal])
    cells=collections.defaultdict(list)
    for b,t,e in items:
        x=(b[0]+b[2])/2;y=(b[1]+b[3])/2
        xwalls=[xs[0],xs[-1]]+[(r[0]+r[2])/2 for r in vertical if r[1]-2<=y<=r[3]+2]
        ywalls=[ys[0],ys[-1]]+[(r[1]+r[3])/2 for r in horizontal if r[0]-2<=x<=r[2]+2]
        left=max(v for v in xwalls if v<x);right=min(v for v in xwalls if v>x)
        top=max(v for v in ywalls if v<y);bottom=min(v for v in ywalls if v>y)
        cells[(left,top,right,bottom)].append((b,t,e))
    # A top band with no drawn wall of its own (a shaded header over a ruled body) holds one cell
    # across the body's columns. When every word in it stands clear inside one column (no word
    # within reach of a body wall) and the words fill two or more columns, the band holds one
    # header per column, not one header repeated across them (tablesub8). A spanning header
    # crosses a wall or reaches it.
    for key in [k for k in cells if k[1]<=ys[0]+2]:
        inner=[x for x in xs if key[0]+2<x<key[2]-2]
        if not inner: continue
        its=cells[key]
        if any(b[0]-.3*height<x<b[2]+.3*height for x in inner for b,t,e in its): continue
        walls=[key[0]]+inner+[key[2]]
        slot=lambda b: next(i for i,(l,r) in enumerate(zip(walls,walls[1:])) if l<(b[0]+b[2])/2<r)
        if len({slot(b) for b,t,e in its})<2: continue
        del cells[key]
        for b,t,e in its:
            i=slot(b); cells[(walls[i],key[1],walls[i+1],key[3])].append((b,t,e))
    out=[];seen_cells=set()
    for a,b in zip(ys,ys[1:]):
        if b-a<height*.4:continue
        row=[];owners=set()
        for l,r in zip(xs,xs[1:]):
            owned=[key for key in cells if key[0]<=(l+r)/2<=key[2] and key[1]<=(a+b)/2<=key[3]]
            if out: owned=[key for key in owned if abs(key[0]-l)<2]
            owners.update(owned);cs=[cells[key] for key in owned]
            row.append([' '.join(t for bb,t,e in L['items']) for its in cs for L in table_lines(its)])
        if any(row) and owners-seen_cells:out.append(row)
        seen_cells.update(owners)
    return out


def table_subrows(rows):
    """A study cell may span several category/definition/value rows. Split that inner three-column grid
    at its aligned category starts, repeating the shared cells rather than cutting their prose into fragments.
    Repeated pairs of short labels beside independent values also identify subrows. Keep their
    shared outer cells on the first printed row; an empty outer cell does not merge the pair."""
    out=[]
    code=re.compile(r'(?:[A-Z]{1,3}|(?:Grade|Level)\s*\d+)')
    pairs=collections.Counter()
    for row in rows:
        if row['header']: continue
        for c,parts in row['cells'].items():
            if (c>0 and len(parts)==2 and len(set(parts))==2 and all(code.fullmatch(t) for t in parts)
                    and len(row['segments'])==2 and all(c in s and c+1 in s for s in row['segments'])):
                pairs[c,tuple(parts)]+=1
    for row in rows:
        cells=row['cells']; segments=row['segments']; split=None
        paired=next((c for c,parts in cells.items() if pairs[c,tuple(parts)]>=2
                     and len(segments)==2 and all(c in s and c+1 in s for s in segments)),None)
        if paired is not None:
            out.extend(dict(row,cells={c:list(ts) for c,ts in segment.items()}) for segment in segments)
            continue
        if not row['header']:
            for c,parts in cells.items():
                if c==0 or len(parts)<2 or not all(code.fullmatch(t) for t in parts): continue
                starts=[i for i,s in enumerate(segments) if c in s]
                groups=[segments[a:b] for a,b in zip(starts,starts[1:]+[len(segments)])]
                # Counts with percentages can start slightly below their category baseline. A paragraph
                # describing several time points is shared text, not one value per category.
                values=[' '.join(t for s in group for t in s.get(c+2,[])).rsplit(':',1)[-1].strip() for group in groups]
                if all(any(c+1 in s for s in group) and re.match(r'^\d+(?:[.,]\d+)?\s*\(', value) for group,value in zip(groups,values)):
                    split=(c,starts); break
        if split is None:
            out.append(row); continue
        c,starts=split
        for a,b in zip(starts,starts[1:]+[len(segments)]):
            child=dict(cells)
            for col in (c,c+1,c+2):
                child[col]=[t for s in segments[a:b] for t in s.get(col,[])]
            out.append(dict(row,cells=child))
    return out


def table_list_rows(lines, graphics, height):
    """A one-column shaded list has entries, not one row per typeset line.

    Lower-case and hanging continuations join their entry at the column's
    normal line pitch. A bullet, new emphasis, sentence end, larger gap or
    intervening shade/rule edge starts an entry. Capitalized aligned entries
    remain separate even when the printer used ordinary leading between them.
    """
    rows=[];previous=None
    steps=sorted(b['vc']-a['vc'] for a,b in zip(lines,lines[1:]) if b['vc']-a['vc']>.6*height)
    pitch=steps[len(steps)//4] if steps else 1.5*height
    for line in lines:
        text=' '.join(t for b,t,e in line['items'])
        left=min(b[0] for b,t,e in line['items'])
        bold=any(re.search(r'bold|black|[-,](?:B|BI)$',face,re.I)
                 for b,t,e in line['items'] for face in e.get('_faces',{}))
        border=previous and any(previous['bot']<=y<=line['top'] for b,k in graphics
            if b[0]<=left<=b[2] for y in ((b[1],b[3]) if k=='fill' else ((b[1]+b[3])/2,)))
        continuation=bool(previous and not border and not bold and not LIST_MARKER.match(text)
            and line['vc']-previous['vc']<=1.15*pitch and not SENT_END.search(rows[-1][0][-1])
            and (re.match(r'^[(["“‘]?[a-z]',text) or left>entry_left+.7*height
                 and re.search(r'[,;\-‐]$',rows[-1][0][-1])))
        if continuation: rows[-1][0].append(text)
        else:
            rows.append(TableRow([[text]],group=bold));entry_left=left
        previous=line
    return rows


def table_cell_bands(lines, graphics, cuts, header_end, separators, height):
    """Prove block-cell row bands without pairing adjacent printed baselines.

    Independent bulleted columns under a header form one body row when every
    column is a hanging list, their item starts are not synchronized, and no
    column has a larger inter-entry gap than its normal leading. For
    shaded prose blocks, each band must start with a bold first-column label,
    followed by ordinary first-column lines. A second bold label in that band
    vetoes it. Numeric/study grids keep their existing observation-row rules.
    Returned intervals are in the table's reading frame, including rotation.
    """
    if header_end is None or not cuts:return []
    column=lambda b: next((i for i,x in enumerate(cuts) if (b[0]+b[2])/2<x),len(cuts))
    body=[L for L in lines if L['vc']>header_end]
    if not body:return []
    columns=[table_lines([it for L in body for it in L['items'] if column(it[0])==c])
             for c in range(len(cuts)+1)]
    bullet=re.compile(r'^[•●◦\x7f-\x9f\u2023\u25aa\uf0b7]\s*')
    item_starts=[]
    for ls in columns:
        starts=[L['vc'] for L in ls if bullet.match(' '.join(t for b,t,e in L['items']))]
        if not ls or len(starts)<2 or not bullet.match(' '.join(t for b,t,e in ls[0]['items'])):break
        margin=min(b[0] for L in ls for b,t,e in L['items'])
        if any(not bullet.match(' '.join(t for b,t,e in L['items'])) and
               min(b[0] for b,t,e in L['items'])<margin+.4*height for L in ls):break
        steps=sorted(b['vc']-a['vc'] for a,b in zip(ls,ls[1:]) if b['vc']-a['vc']>.6*height)
        if steps and steps[-1]>1.2*steps[len(steps)//4]:break
        item_starts.append(starts)
    else:
        interior=[y for y in separators if body[0]['bot']<y<body[-1]['top']]
        asynchronous=any(not any(abs(y-z)<.5*height for z in item_starts[0])
                         for starts in item_starts[1:] for y in starts)
        if not interior and asynchronous:
            return [(header_end,body[-1]['bot']+height)]
    # The first label must be distinguished from its contents by typography;
    # shading alone can span several independent observations in a data grid.
    edges=sorted({header_end,body[-1]['bot']+height}|{y for y in separators if y>header_end})
    bands=[]
    for top,bottom in zip(edges,edges[1:]):
        ls=[L for L in columns[0] if top<=L['vc']<bottom]
        if not ls:continue
        def bold(L):
            return any(re.search(r'bold|black|[-,](?:B|BI)$',face,re.I)
                       for b,t,e in L['items'] for face in e.get('_faces',{}))
        if len(ls)<3 or not bold(ls[0]) or any(bold(L) for L in ls[1:]):return []
        if not any(top<=L['vc']<bottom for col in columns[1:] for L in col):return []
        bands.append((top,bottom))
    return bands if len(bands)>=2 else []


def table_entry_bands(lines, cuts, header_end, height, publication=False, regions=None, study=False):
    """Citation starts in a bibliographic first column delimit whole entries.

    The citation may wrap over five lines; a slash joining two references
    keeps both in one entry. Country/title lines belong to that publication.
    Comma-separated coauthors and a subsample/follow-up reference stay together.
    An explicitly headed narrative study column uses the same entry ownership:
    country, arms, bullets and comments continue until the next primary citation.
    A contiguous secondary bibliography introduced by "also reported in" stays
    with its study. Other columns retain the tall-band safeguard. Separate category
    labels before a citation retain their own bands. No neighboring column supplies row keys.
    """
    if header_end is None or not cuts: return []
    first=table_lines([it for L in lines if L['vc']>header_end
                       for it in L['items'] if (it[0][0]+it[0][2])/2<cuts[0]])
    import unicodedata
    citation=re.compile(r'^(?:(?:(?:de|da|del|dos|van|von)\s+)*[A-ZÀ-ÖØ-Þ][\w’\'‐-]+,?(?:\s+(?:and|&|de|da|van|von|[A-ZÀ-ÖØ-Þ][\w’\'‐-]+,?))*\s+)'
                        r'(?:et\s+al\.?\s*\(?\d|(?:and|&)\s+[A-ZÀ-ÖØ-Þ][\w’\'‐-]+\s*\d|\([12]\d{3})')
    texts=[unicodedata.normalize('NFC',' '.join(t for b,t,e in L['items'])) for L in first]
    starts=[];consumed=-1;secondary=False
    for i,L in enumerate(first):
        if study:
            if i and L['top']-first[i-1]['bot']>height:secondary=False
            if re.match(r'^(?:also\s+)?reported\s+in\b|^see\s+also\b',texts[i],re.I):secondary=True
            if secondary:continue
        if i<=consumed or i and re.search(r'/[\w ]*$',texts[i-1]): continue
        if i and L['top']-first[i-1]['bot']<=height and (
                re.search(r'\b(?:of|from)\s*$|\bF[-‐]up\s*$',texts[i-1],re.I)
                or texts[i-1].count('(')>texts[i-1].count(')')): continue
        for end in range(i+1,min(len(first),i+5)+1):
            if end>i+1 and first[end-1]['top']-first[end-2]['bot']>height: break
            # A complete following citation cannot recruit the last country
            # line of an already open entry into its author name. Explicit
            # coauthor joins still allow wrapped bibliographic names. The
            # following citation may itself wrap ("Name," / "Name, Name," /
            # "(year):"), so it is complete when it starts on any later line
            # of the window, not only on the last one.
            if (study and end>i+1 and i and L['top']-first[i-1]['bot']<=height
                    and any(citation.search(' '.join(texts[k:end])) and not re.search(r'[,;&\-‐]|\band$',texts[k-1])
                            for k in range(i+1,end))):break
            if citation.search(' '.join(texts[i:end])):
                starts.append(L['top']-.2*height);consumed=end-1;break
    if len(starts)<2 and not ((publication or study) and len(starts)==1 and len(first)>=3): return []
    if regions is not None and not any(sum(lo<=y<hi for y in starts)>=2 for lo,hi in regions): return []
    # Once citations establish the column's role, a separated first-cell
    # block is also an entry. This protects unfamiliar names or labels from
    # being swallowed merely because their citation spelling was not matched.
    for i,L in enumerate(first[1:],1):
        if (L['top']-first[i-1]['bot']>1.2*height and not re.search(r'/[\w ]*$',texts[i-1])
                and not re.match(r'^(?:Subsample|Continuation|Follow[- ]?up|(?:also\s+)?reported\s+in|see\s+also)\b',texts[i],re.I)):
            starts.append(L['top']-.2*height)
    # A solitary non-citation first-cell line separated from the preceding
    # reference by a blank band is a category, not part of that reference.
    if not publication:
        for i,L in enumerate(first):
            if any(abs(L['top']-.2*height-y)<.1 for y in starts): continue
            if (i==0 or L['top']-first[i-1]['bot']>height) and i+1<len(first) and any(
                    abs(first[i+1]['top']-.2*height-y)<.1 for y in starts):
                starts.append(L['top']-.2*height)
    # Two label-only lines (nothing on their baselines but text set from the first column) are two
    # rows when the second stands a row step below the first, wider than the step of the column's
    # wrapped lines by .3 line height, and is indented from it or set in a face it does not use (r3-labelrows: a group
    # heading and the indented subheading under it). A label wrapped at the line step stays one cell.
    if study and len(first)>=3:
        runs_start={}
        for K in lines:
            for b,t,e in K['items']: runs_start[id(e)]=min(runs_start.get(id(e),b[0]),b[0])
        def label_only(F):
            same=[it for K in lines if abs(K['vc']-F['vc'])<.3*height for it in K['items']]
            return bool(same) and all(runs_start[id(e)]<cuts[0] for b,t,e in same)
        face_set=lambda F: {re.sub(r'^[A-Z]{6}\+','',face) for b,t,e in F['items'] for face in e.get('_faces',{})}
        # the wrapped-line step: the smallest step between first-column lines, recurring at least twice
        steps=sorted(b['top']-a['top'] for a,b in itertools.pairwise(first) if b['top']-a['top']>.6*height)
        wrap=steps[0] if len(steps)>=2 and steps[1]-steps[0]<.15*height else None
        for K,L in itertools.pairwise(first):
            if wrap is None or any(abs(L['top']-.2*height-y)<.1 for y in starts): continue
            if L['top']-K['top']<wrap+.3*height or not (label_only(K) and label_only(L)): continue
            x_K=min(b[0] for b,t,e in K['items']); x_L=min(b[0] for b,t,e in L['items'])
            if x_L>x_K+.5*height or (face_set(K) and face_set(L) and not face_set(L)<=face_set(K)):
                starts.append(L['top']-.2*height)
    starts=sorted(set([header_end]+starts))
    return list(zip(starts,starts[1:]+[lines[-1]['bot']+height]))


def ligature_reading(font, code, before, decode_name):
    """Recover a truncated encoding name only when ToUnicode is absent.

    The reader's single-character encoding cache clips multi-letter AGL
    names such as T_h. Require the selected embedded glyph and encoding name
    to agree, and require the current reading to be exactly the first letter.
    Explicit Unicode mappings, ActualText, generic names, synthetic TrueType
    names and already expanded glyphs retain their authoritative readings.
    """
    if not isinstance(code, int) or len(before) != 1 or not before.isalpha(): return None
    if font.prog is None or font._tounicode(code): return None
    name = font.selected_glyph_name(code)
    if not name or name != font.adobe_name(code): return None
    full = decode_name(name)
    if (not 2 <= len(full) <= 4 or not full.isascii() or not full.isalpha() or
            full[0] != before or font.unicode(code) != before): return None
    return full


def restore_ligature_text(pages, uncertain):
    """Expand named ligatures in private prose/table views, preserving glyph IDs.

    Each edit revalidates the whole run against its native character IDs and
    the PDF font/code. Font dictionaries sharing a subset name must agree.
    Spaces and glyph geometry are unchanged. Saved boxer JSON stays native;
    copy_prose_glyphs and page notices retain the recovery's provenance.
    """
    reader = sys.modules.get('_copy_table_reader')
    if reader is None: return
    for page in pages:
        chars = page.get('_table_chars', [])
        pdf = page.get('_table_pdf')
        if not chars or not pdf: continue
        fonts = collections.defaultdict(list)
        for font in reader.sc_doc(pdf).fonts.values(): fonts[font.basefont].append(font)
        cache = {}; replacements = {}; changed = []
        for e in page['elements']:
            if e.get('kind') != 'text': continue
            ids = e.get('char_ids', [])
            if not ids or any(not isinstance(i, int) or not 0 <= i < len(chars) for i in ids): continue
            expanded = [(i, ch) for i in ids for ch in chars[i]['text'] if not ch.isspace()]
            positions = [(i, ch) for i,ch in enumerate(e['text']) if not ch.isspace()]
            if [ch for _,ch in expanded] != [ch for _,ch in positions]: continue
            new = list(e['text']); edits = []
            for (offset,before),(cid,_) in zip(positions,expanded):
                c = chars[cid]; key = c['font'], c.get('source_code'), c['text']
                if len(c['text']) != 1: continue
                if key not in cache:
                    readings = [ligature_reading(f,key[1],key[2],reader.sc_uni) for f in fonts[key[0]]]
                    cache[key] = readings[0] if readings and all(r == readings[0] for r in readings) else None
                after = cache[key]
                if not after: continue
                new[offset] = after; replacements[cid] = after
                edits.append(dict(offset=offset,char_id=cid,font=key[0],source_code=key[1],before=before,after=after))
            if edits:
                before = e['text']; e['text'] = ''.join(new)
                page.setdefault('copy_prose_glyphs', []).append(dict(run=e['id'], before=before, after=e['text'],
                    why='full embedded ligature name recovered from truncated font encoding', glyphs=edits))
                changed.extend(edits)
        if replacements:
            page['_table_chars'] = [dict(c,text=replacements[i]) if i in replacements else c for i,c in enumerate(chars)]
            for e in page['elements']:
                if e.get('_chars'): e['_chars'] = [page['_table_chars'][i] for i in e['char_ids']]
        if changed:
            mappings = ', '.join(dict.fromkeys(repr(s['before'])+' → '+repr(s['after']) for s in changed))
            uncertain.append(f"page {page['page']}: {len(changed)} truncated ligatures restored from embedded glyph names ({mappings}); check the printed glyphs")


def sign_text_runs(pages, uncertain):
    """Apply provisional OCR only at U+FFFD positions, before prose/table assembly.
    Keep the verified character geometry and typography: update this copy's
    character dictionaries as well as run text, so table_words still verifies
    its IDs and splits cells at the original glyph boxes. Saved JSON and reader
    caches retain native text. Report each page's substitutions and minimum TSV
    confidence without inserting visible markers into values or words.
    """
    for page in pages:
        subs = []
        chars = page.get('_table_chars', [])
        replacements = {}
        for e in page['elements']:
            old, new = e.get('text', ''), e.get('ocr_text')
            if e.get('kind') != 'text' or not isinstance(new, str) or len(old) != len(new): continue
            edits = [(i, b) for i, (a, b) in enumerate(zip(old, new)) if a != b]
            metadata = e.get('ocr_subs', [])
            if not edits or any(old[i] != '�' or b.isspace() or b == '�' for i, b in edits): continue
            if [(s.get('offset'), s.get('after')) for s in metadata] != edits: continue
            if any(not isinstance(s.get('confidence'), (int, float)) or not 0 <= s['confidence'] <= 100 for s in metadata): continue
            # Refuse stale IDs rather than losing a table's character-level split.
            ids = e.get('char_ids', [])
            indexed = [i for i in ids for ch in chars[i]['text'] if not ch.isspace()] if ids and chars and min(ids)>=0 and max(ids)<len(chars) else []
            positions = [i for i,ch in enumerate(old) if not ch.isspace()]
            if chars and (len(indexed)!=len(positions) or
                          re.sub(r'\s','',old) != ''.join(re.sub(r'\s','',chars[i]['text']) for i in ids)):
                continue
            by_position = dict(zip(positions,indexed))
            if chars and any(by_position.get(s['offset'])!=s.get('char_id') for s in metadata): continue
            for s in metadata:
                if chars: replacements[s['char_id']] = s['after']
            e['text'] = new
            subs.extend(metadata)
        if replacements:
            page['_table_chars'] = [dict(c, text=replacements[i]) if i in replacements else c for i,c in enumerate(chars)]
            for e in page['elements']:
                if e.get('_chars'):
                    e['_chars'] = [page['_table_chars'][i] for i in e['char_ids'] if page['_table_chars'][i]['text'].strip()]
        if subs:
            signs = ', '.join(dict.fromkeys(s['after'] for s in subs))
            uncertain.append(f"page {page['page']}: {len(subs)} signs read by OCR ({signs}): check them; "
                             f"lowest confidence {min(s['confidence'] for s in subs):.2f}/100")


def private_use_code(ch):
    import unicodedata
    return unicodedata.category(ch) == 'Co'


def replace_private_codes(pages, authors, uncertain):
    """No private-use code reaches a copy: each one left after the glyph checks is shown as U+FFFD.

    check_fonts.py reads private-use codes of Symbol fonts through the Adobe
    Symbol encoding and the copy hook applies the verified readings first. A
    code still private here (another font's code, a stale or unverifiable
    decision, a run whose character IDs do not match) has no verified reading.
    Run text and the private table characters change together; saved JSON and
    reader caches stay native. Rule 8's title, author and address strings get
    the same readings: a code read one way everywhere in the runs takes that
    reading, any other code U+FFFD. Each replacement is listed per page/font.
    """
    readings = collections.defaultdict(set)
    for p in pages:
        for before, after in p.pop('_private_readings', []): readings[before].add(after)
    places = collections.Counter()
    for p in pages:
        chars = p.get('_table_chars', []); replacements = {}
        for e in p['elements']:
            if e.get('kind') != 'text' or not any(private_use_code(ch) for ch in e.get('text', '')): continue
            spans = e.get('font_spans') if isinstance(e.get('font_spans'), list) else []
            for i, ch in enumerate(e['text']):
                if private_use_code(ch):
                    font = next((s['font'] for s in spans if s['start'] <= i < s['end']), 'unknown font')
                    places[p['page'], font, ch] += 1
            e['text'] = ''.join('�' if private_use_code(ch) else ch for ch in e['text'])
        for i, c in enumerate(chars):
            if any(private_use_code(ch) for ch in c.get('text', '')):
                replacements[i] = ''.join('�' if private_use_code(ch) else ch for ch in c['text'])
        if replacements:
            p['_table_chars'] = [dict(c, text=replacements[i]) if i in replacements else c for i, c in enumerate(chars)]
            for e in p['elements']:
                if e.get('_chars') and any(i in replacements for i in e.get('char_ids', [])):
                    e['_chars'] = [p['_table_chars'][i] for i in e['char_ids'] if p['_table_chars'][i]['text'].strip()]
    def translate(value):
        if isinstance(value, str):
            return ''.join((next(iter(readings[ch])) if len(readings.get(ch, ())) == 1 else '�')
                           if private_use_code(ch) else ch for ch in value)
        if isinstance(value, list): return [translate(v) for v in value]
        if isinstance(value, dict): return {k: translate(v) for k, v in value.items()}
        return value
    if authors: authors.update(translate(dict(authors)))
    grouped = collections.defaultdict(collections.Counter)
    for (page, font, ch), n in places.items(): grouped[page, font][f'U+{ord(ch):04X}'] += n
    for (page, font), codes in sorted(grouped.items()):
        uncertain.append(f"page {page}: font {font}: {sum(codes.values())} private-use code(s) without a verified "
                         f"reading ({', '.join(f'{k} {v}' for k, v in sorted(codes.items()))}) shown as �; check the printed glyphs")


def shape_text_runs(page, member_runs):
    """Text the PDF draws as shapes (vector outlines, no characters) inside a table: the element boxer records
    each such run as an "outline" element with its box and glyph count. Each one becomes a cell holding a marked
    gap unless the optional read_shapes step supplied nonempty ocr_text. Joined fragments are emitted
    once at their combined box. OCR is printed with [ocr] and a check warning, including the lowest
    available word confidence (0–100); it is not promoted to PDF text. A touching native suffix that
    starts with punctuation is kept attached to the outline word, with the marker after the combined
    text. That native run is removed from member_runs to prevent duplication.
    Returns (pseudo-runs, table warning).
    (Measured: N05 Tables 2 and 4, whose category labels are outlines.)"""
    if not member_runs: return [], ""
    H = hull(e["box_px"] for e in member_runs)
    ang = collections.Counter(round(e["angle"]) % 360 for e in member_runs).most_common(1)[0][0]
    out, read, confidences = [], 0, []
    elements = {e['id']: e for e in page.get('elements', [])}
    for e in page.get("elements", []):
        if e.get("kind") != "outline": continue
        if e.get("overlay"): continue   # R2 (lane stream): pen strokes in a colour off the page's layout ink are no printed text
        owner = elements.get(e.get('ocr_joined_to'), {})
        if isinstance(owner.get('ocr_text'), str) and owner['ocr_text'].strip(): continue
        ocr = e.get("ocr_text")
        ocr = " ".join(ocr.split()) if isinstance(ocr, str) else ""
        b = e.get('ocr_box_px', e['box_px']) if ocr else e['box_px']
        if b[0] >= H[0] - 2 and b[2] <= H[2] + 2 and b[1] >= H[1] - 2 and b[3] <= H[3] + 2:
            n = e.get("count") or 0
            read += bool(ocr)
            confidence = e.get('ocr_conf')
            if ocr and isinstance(confidence, (int, float)) and 0 <= confidence <= 100:
                confidences.append(confidence)
            if ocr and ang == 0:
                h = b[3] - b[1]
                suffixes = [r for r in member_runs if re.match(r'^[+/−]', r.get('text', ''))
                            and round(r['angle']) % 360 == ang
                            and 0 <= r['box_px'][0] - b[2] <= .25 * h
                            and abs(sum(r['box_px'][1::2]) - (b[1] + b[3])) <= .6 * h]
                if len(suffixes) == 1:
                    suffix = suffixes[0]
                    ocr += suffix['text']
                    b = hull([b, suffix['box_px']])
                    member_runs.remove(suffix)
            text = ocr + " [ocr]" if ocr else f"[unreadable:{n}-letters-drawn-as-shapes]"
            out.append({"id": e["id"], "kind": "text", "text": text, "box_px": list(b),
                        "angle": ang, "line": 100000 + len(out), "char_ids": [], "size_px": b[3] - b[1], "flags": []})
    confidence_note = f"lowest confidence {min(confidences):.2f}/100" if confidences else "confidence unavailable"
    if confidences and len(confidences) < read:
        confidence_note += f"; confidence unavailable for {read - len(confidences)} label(s)"
    warnings = [f"{read} label(s) read by OCR; {confidence_note}; check them"] if read else []
    if len(out) > read:
        warnings.append(f"{len(out) - read} label(s) are drawn as shapes, not text, and could not be read; they are marked [unreadable] in their cells")
    return out, "; ".join(warnings)


def own_header_columns(lines, x, lo=None, hi=None):
    """Adjacent header cells with their own data columns stay separate columns (AP06 Table 2).
    When every data row fills only one of two neighbouring columns (the values alternate between
    them), no data line pairs across their gutter and the paired-line vote drops it. The header
    still proves the gutter: the first lines holding cells on both sides of x (between the
    neighbouring gutters lo and hi) are header cells; no line from the first of them down
    crosses x; and on each side at least two later lines hold a value centred under that side's
    header cells. Two is the recurrence the paired-line vote itself asks of a gutter, so one
    stray value cannot open a column. The values must alternate: below the header cells, some
    line holds a value on the left side only and some line on the right side only, and such
    one-sided lines outnumber lines holding values on both sides. A column pair whose rows are
    mostly filled on both sides, or whose lone values always fall on the same side (an estimate
    beside its interval with a reference level, which the vote joins on purpose), is left to the
    vote. One header line
    is enough, so the same proof also keeps the gutter among the vote's candidates, where the
    pair and independence recurrence would need two lines."""
    sides=[];crossed=[];paired=[]
    for i,L in enumerate(lines):
        left=[(b,e) for b,t,e in L['items'] if b[2]<=x and (lo is None or b[0]>lo)]
        right=[(b,e) for b,t,e in L['items'] if b[0]>=x and (hi is None or b[2]<hi)]
        sides.append(([b for b,e in left],[b for b,e in right]))
        crossed.append(any(b[0]+1<x<b[2]-1 for b,t,e in L['items']))
        # A pair is two cells: the words beside the channel belong to different saved runs.
        if left and right and not crossed[-1] and max(left,key=lambda be:be[0][2])[1] is not min(right,key=lambda be:be[0][0])[1]:
            paired.append(i)
    if not paired or any(crossed[paired[0]:]): return False
    first_value=next((i for i in range(paired[0]+1,len(lines)) if i not in paired and any(sides[i])),None)
    if first_value is None: return False
    heads=[i for i in paired if i<first_value]
    left_only=sum(bool(sides[i][0]) and not sides[i][1] for i in range(first_value,len(lines)))
    right_only=sum(bool(sides[i][1]) and not sides[i][0] for i in range(first_value,len(lines)))
    if not left_only or not right_only or len(paired)-len(heads)>=left_only+right_only: return False
    lh=hull(b for i in heads for b in sides[i][0]); rh=hull(b for i in heads for b in sides[i][1])
    under=lambda bs,hb: any(hb[0]<=(b[0]+b[2])/2<=hb[2] for b in bs)
    left_values=sum(under(sides[i][0],lh) for i in range(first_value,len(lines)))
    right_values=sum(under(sides[i][1],rh) for i in range(first_value,len(lines)))
    return left_values>=2 and right_values>=2

def table_word_space(lines, h):
    """The table's word space: the median white between neighbouring words narrower than half a line height."""
    gaps=sorted(g for L in lines for (a,_,_),(b,_,_) in zip(L['items'],L['items'][1:]) for g in [b[0]-a[2]] if 0<g<.5*h)
    return gaps[len(gaps)//2] if gaps else .25*h

def table_phrases(boxes, space):
    """Boxes in reading order joined into phrases: words set at most two word spaces apart."""
    phrases=[]
    for b in boxes:
        if phrases and b[0]-phrases[-1][2]<=2*space: phrases[-1]=hull([phrases[-1],b])
        else: phrases.append(list(b))
    return phrases

def paired_header_columns(lines, x, h, lo=None, hi=None, heads=None, texts=None, known=()):
    """Two neighbouring columns filled on the same lines stay two columns (r2-cells). The vote
    joins a gutter that most lines cross from a number on its left to a bracket on its right,
    since an estimate printed beside its interval is normally one cell. Separate header cells
    above the two sides, each heading its own aligned column, prove two columns instead. The
    proof rests on the header pair and the alignment of the two sides, never on saved runs (a
    producer may save an estimate and its interval as one run) or on a line-height channel
    (a right-aligned estimate beside a left-aligned interval leaves a narrow one):
    - the table's word space is the median white between neighbouring words narrower than half
      a line height; a channel is white at x wider than two word spaces, and a line whose words
      meet at x with a word space, or a word across x, joins the two sides (one phrase);
    - the header pair is the first line with words on both sides of x (between the neighbouring
      gutters lo and hi) and a channel between them, each side holding a word;
    - above it, only labels stand between the gutters: a phrase (words a word space apart)
      reaching over both header cells, or a shorter one sharing no edge and no centre with
      either header cell (a group label over the pair or a wider group), or one reaching over a
      neighbouring gutter (a title); a label aligned with one header cell is that cell's first
      line (a wrapped header), so the pair is not proven;
    - from the header pair down no line joins the two sides; at least two lines below it hold
      words on both sides (each with its channel, so the channel is consistent down the rows);
    - on each side those lines share one edge or the centre with that side's header cell, and
      neither header cell's text recurs on most of those lines (a repeated label is no header).
    Lines filled on one side only (a reference level) neither prove nor refute the pair. A header
    set as one phrase over both sides ("OR (95% CI)") or values set with a word space never meet
    the proof. The header-pair line is appended to heads."""
    space=table_word_space(lines,h)
    within=lambda b: (lo is None or b[0]>lo) and (hi is None or b[2]<hi)
    # A subgroup heading row: one phrase that starts at the table's left text edge and runs on across
    # x (a heading over the value columns, as in summary-of-findings tables). Like a line filled on
    # one side only, it neither proves nor refutes the pair below its header.
    margin=min((b[0] for L in lines for b,t,e in L['items']),default=0)
    def heading(L):
        ph=table_phrases([b for b,t,e in L['items']],space)
        return len(ph)==1 and ph[0][0]<margin+.5*h and ph[0][0]<x<ph[0][2]
    sides=[];joined=[]
    for L in lines:
        if heading(L):
            sides.append(([],[])); joined.append(False); continue
        left=[b for b,t,e in L['items'] if b[2]<=x and (lo is None or b[0]>lo)]
        right=[b for b,t,e in L['items'] if b[0]>=x and (hi is None or b[2]<hi)]
        sides.append((left,right))
        across=any(b[0]+1<x<b[2]-1 for b,t,e in L['items'])
        joined.append(across or bool(left and right and min(b[0] for b in right)-max(b[2] for b in left)<=2*space))
    both=[i for i,(l,r) in enumerate(sides) if l and r and not joined[i]]
    if not both or any(joined[both[0]:]): return False
    head,rows=both[0],both[1:]
    lh=hull(sides[head][0]); rh=hull(sides[head][1])
    # A header cell holds a word or a symbol header ('%', 'n', 'P'), not a bare number.
    word=lambda bs: any(re.search(r'[^\W\d_]|[%#‰§]',t) and not re.fullmatch(r'[\d\s.,·:/<>≤≥±+\-−–—]*',t) for b,t,e in lines[head]['items'] if any(b is bb for bb in bs))
    if not (word(sides[head][0]) and word(sides[head][1])): return False
    # A header cell heads values different from itself: a side whose text recurs on most rows below
    # is a repeated label ("Level I", "Level II"), not a header over a column (a header line
    # repeated once inside the table is still a header).
    text=lambda i,k: ' '.join(t for b,t,e in lines[i]['items'] if any(b is bb for bb in sides[i][k]))
    if any(2*sum(text(i,k)==text(head,k) for i in rows)>len(rows) for k in (0,1)): return False
    # Labels above the header pair over one side only that share an edge or the centre with a header
    # cell: they are that cell's wrapped header lines when they stack (two or more such lines) or when
    # one stands at the header's own leading right above it (at most one and a half line heights);
    # a single aligned label set apart above the header is a group label over the pair (w1 form,
    # r2-cells round 5). The same header pair proven elsewhere in this table (known) outweighs the
    # wrapped reading.
    mid=lambda b: (b[0]+b[2])/2
    def first_line(p):
        return any(abs(p[0]-c[0])<.3*h or abs(p[2]-c[2])<.3*h or abs(mid(p)-mid(c))<.3*h for c in (lh,rh))
    aligned_lines=[i for i in range(head)
                   if any(within(p) and not (p[0]<lh[2] and p[2]>rh[0]) and first_line(p)
                          for p in table_phrases([b for b,t,e in lines[i]['items']],space))]
    wrapped=len(aligned_lines)>=2 or any(lines[head]['vc']-lines[i]['vc']<=1.5*h for i in aligned_lines)
    if (text(head,0),text(head,1)) not in known and wrapped: return False
    if len(rows)<2: return False
    def aligned(k):
        hb=hull(sides[head][k]); boxes=[hull(sides[i][k]) for i in rows]
        return (all(abs(b[0]-hb[0])<.3*h for b in boxes) or all(abs(b[2]-hb[2])<.3*h for b in boxes)
                or all(abs((b[0]+b[2])/2-(hb[0]+hb[2])/2)<.3*h for b in boxes))
    if not (aligned(0) and aligned(1)): return False
    if heads is not None: heads.append(lines[head])   # the caller learns the header-pair line
    if texts is not None: texts.append((text(head,0),text(head,1)))
    return True

def value_stack(lines, x, h, hi=None):
    """A first column of labels beside a stack of values stays two columns (tablesub6; AS08 Table 4).
    When the labels wrap or are raised off their values' baselines, few lines hold a label and a value
    at once, and the paired-line vote drops the gutter: the values then fall into the label column.
    The page still proves the gutter by its geometry: no line crosses x; from the first line holding
    a value right of x (before the next gutter hi) every line's text there is a complete value, on at
    least two lines, with a shared left edge, right edge or centre (one stacked column); left of x at least two
    lines hold words starting at the table's left edge and none holds a value; and wherever a line
    holds both, more than a line height of white lies between them (a gutter, not a word space)."""
    value=lambda s: bool(re.search(r'\d',s)) and not re.search(r'[^\W\d_]{2}',s)
    margin=min(b[0] for L in lines for b,t,e in L['items'])
    right_vals=[];labels=0;started=False
    for L in lines:
        its=L['items']
        if any(b[0]+1<x<b[2]-1 for b,t,e in its): return False
        left=[(b,t) for b,t,e in its if b[2]<=x]
        right=[(b,t) for b,t,e in its if b[0]>=x and (hi is None or b[2]<=hi)]
        rt=' '.join(t for b,t in right).strip(); lt=' '.join(t for b,t in left).strip()
        if right and value(rt): started=True
        if started and right:
            if not value(rt): return False
            right_vals.append(hull(b for b,t in right))
        if left:
            if value(lt): return False
            if re.search(r'[^\W\d_]{2}',lt) and min(b[0] for b,t in left)<margin+.5*h: labels+=1
            if right and min(b[0] for b,t in right)-max(b[2] for b,t in left)<=h: return False
    if len(right_vals)<2 or labels<2: return False
    spread=lambda xs: max(xs)-min(xs)<.3*h
    return spread([b[0] for b in right_vals]) or spread([b[2] for b in right_vals]) or spread([(b[0]+b[2])/2 for b in right_vals])

def table_rows(runs, body, page=None):
    """Reconstruct cells from gutters, PDF character positions and drawn row structure, in the table's frame.
    Repeated fill edges and segmented rules support column boundaries. Distinct header cells retain
    sparse columns even when their data seldom share a baseline. A clear channel between those cells
    has no minimum width; leaf headers beneath a spanning underline remain distinct even when
    their values alternate between columns. A nearby closing border retains full-height hierarchy
    dividers below vertically centred labels. Repeated left edges split a saved
    run spanning cells without moving the preceding cell's overhanging final word. Passing an indented
    start within the same column is not a cell boundary. Wide rules/fill edges delimit rows;
    within unruled bands, first-column starts, vertical gaps measured within columns and aligned populated
    cells distinguish new rows from wrapped cells. Line pitch is measured within columns so staggered
    text does not invent row gaps. In narrative study tables, primary first-column citations delimit
    whole studies, including countries, treatment arms and comments. Contiguous secondary citations
    introduced by "also reported in" belong to that same study. Numeric observation matrices retain
    their subrows; a missing first cell alone never proves continuation. Separate panels may have their
    own column grid. Spanning headers occupy separate rows above their subheaders, repeated only across the
    columns covered by their underline or cell background. Notes/continuation labels
    are returned outside the grid. A study/reference table's matching first/last-column bands keep its
    wrapped observations in the study row. Group rows carry an explicit marker for bold rendering.
    Independent hanging bullet lists share a single body row. Shaded prose bands
    beginning with bold labels keep all their cell lines together. One-column
    lists join wrapped continuations while retaining independent entry starts.
    A capitalized first-column continuation can finish an already wrapped name:
    its preceding line starts lowercase and ends with an unfinished word, its
    indent and pitch agree, and the other cells only continue existing lists.
    Flowing notes start at the table margin or follow a bottom border, large gap or font change;
    a wide final column is still a cell.
    None between output rows denotes a new panel, not an empty data row."""
    runs = [e for e in runs if e['text'].strip()]
    if not runs: return [], []
    ang = collections.Counter(round(e['angle'])%360 for e in runs).most_common(1)[0][0]; f=frame(ang)
    # Large watermark glyphs can make rule 1 join ordinary characters from different baselines.
    # Only verified source characters may undo that join. Their median size distinguishes the
